namespace Death {
//###==##====#=====--==~--~=~- --- -- -  -  -   -

/** @page consoles Building the project for consoles
@brief Guide how to build, deploy and run Jazz² Resurrection on game consoles

@tableofcontents

@m_class{m-noindent}

The game runs on ten consoles. Each is cross-compiled with its own SDK, most use a bespoke
window/input backend instead of **SDL2** or **GLFW**, and seven have no programmable shaders, so they
use one of six fixed-function rendering backends @m_span{m-text m-dim} (the Wii and the GameCube share
one) @m_endspan. This page covers installing the toolchain, configuring, packaging, deploying and
getting a log out for each of them. For everything else see @ref building "Building the project".

Console | Toolchain @m_span{m-text m-dim} (CMake toolchain file) @m_endspan | Rendering backend | Window backend | Build artifact
------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- | -------------- | --------------
@ref consoles-n64 "Nintendo 64" | libdragon @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`cmake/toolchains/n64.cmake`) @m_endspan | `RDP` --- fixed-function | `N64` | `jazz2.z64` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (bootable ROM) @m_endspan
@ref consoles-dreamcast "Sega Dreamcast" | KallistiOS @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`kallistios.toolchain.cmake`) @m_endspan | `PVR` --- fixed-function | `Dc` | `jazz2.cdi` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (bootable disc image) @m_endspan
@ref consoles-wii "Nintendo Wii" | devkitPPC + libogc @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`Wii.cmake`) @m_endspan | `GX` --- fixed-function | `Ogc` | `boot.dol` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (in a staged `sd/` tree) @m_endspan
@ref consoles-gamecube "Nintendo GameCube" | devkitPPC + libogc @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`GameCube.cmake`) @m_endspan | `GX` --- fixed-function | `Ogc` | `jazz2.iso` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (bootable disc image) @m_endspan and `Jazz2.dol` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (in a staged `sd/` tree) @m_endspan
@ref consoles-3ds "Nintendo 3DS" | devkitARM + libctru + citro3d @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`3DS.cmake`) @m_endspan | `PICA` --- fixed-function | `Ctr` | `Jazz2.3dsx` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (in a staged `sdmc/` tree) @m_endspan
@ref consoles-psp "PlayStation Portable" | pspdev @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`pspdev.cmake`) @m_endspan | `GU` --- fixed-function | `Psp` | `EBOOT.PBP` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (in a staged `ms0/` tree) @m_endspan
@ref consoles-ps2 "PlayStation 2" | ps2dev @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`ps2dev.cmake`) @m_endspan | `GS` --- fixed-function | `Ps2` | `jazz2.iso` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (bootable disc image) @m_endspan
@ref consoles-ps3 "PlayStation 3" | ps3toolchain + PSL1GHT @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`cmake/toolchains/ps3dev.cmake`) @m_endspan | `RSX` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (native libgcm, <b>shaders</b>) @m_endspan | `Ps3` | `jazz2.pkg` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (and an unsigned `jazz2.self`) @m_endspan
@ref consoles-vita "PlayStation Vita" | VitaSDK @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`vita.toolchain.cmake`) @m_endspan | `GXM` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (native sceGxm, default) @m_endspan, `OpenGL` via **vitaGL** @m_span{m-text m-dim} (ES 2.0 profile) @m_endspan or `Software` | SDL2 | `jazz2.vpk`
@ref consoles-switch "Nintendo Switch" | devkitA64 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`Switch.cmake`) @m_endspan | `OpenGL` | SDL2 | `jazz2.nro`

@m_class{m-noindent}

On the first eight `NCINE_PREFERRED_RHI` is pinned to the console's one backend and any other value
is a configure error. The PlayStation 3's `RSX` is a full shader backend, so it keeps the
post-processing chain and differs from desktop mainly in <em>when</em> its shaders are compiled
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref consoles-ps3) @m_endspan. The PS Vita and the Switch are ordinary
shader platforms; for them mostly the deployment parts apply.

@section consoles-common What every console build shares

-   <b>The host tools are not built.</b> @ref shader-compiler "ShaderCompiler" and
    @ref asset-packer "AssetPacker" are skipped for cross-compiled targets. The generated shader
    headers are committed to the repository; the game content is prepared ahead of time.
-   <b>The game data is converted in advance</b> with AssetPacker. The Nintendo 64, Dreamcast,
    PlayStation 2 and GameCube have no first-run conversion compiled in
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`NCINE_HAS_WRITABLE_CACHE` in @cpp "Sources/Main.h" @ce) @m_endspan and
    don't verify their content, so a build without a prepared tree reaches the main menu with no
    episode to play. The Wii, 3DS, PSP, PS3, Vita and Switch can convert on the device, but skip it when
    they find a prepared tree.
-   <b>Seven of them have no shaders.</b> `RDP`, `PVR`, `GX`, `GU`, `GS` and `PICA` implement each
    effect as fixed-function passes transpiled by ShaderCompiler from the same `.shader` files ---
    see @ref shader-compiler-fixed-function "console fixed-function blocks". There is no
    post-processing chain and no rescale filter; the level viewport is aspect-fitted into the
    console's native output.

@subsection consoles-common-content Preparing the game content

All data-prepared consoles consume the <b>same</b> tree, prepared once with
@ref asset-packer "AssetPacker" and pointed at from each console build. Only the cinematics differ:
the @ref consoles-dreamcast-content "Dreamcast" and the GameCube need their own profile, and the
@ref consoles-n64-content "Nintendo 64" needs its own conversion that also covers sound and music.
The `dreamcast`, `ps2`, `gamecube` and `n64` profiles also write sprite sheets and tilesets in LZ4
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref asset-packer-lz4) @m_endspan; the generic `console` tree keeps the
game's own format.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par What this needs beforehand
An installed copy of the <b>original Jazz Jackrabbit 2</b> --- any retail version or the Shareware
Demo @m_span{m-text m-dim} (which ships @cpp "AnimsSw.j2a" @ce instead of @cpp "Anims.j2a" @ce)
@m_endspan. The game's own half of the tree is the repository's @cpp "Content" @ce directory.

@m_class{m-noindent}

<b>1. Build the tool.</b> It is part of any desktop build @m_span{m-text m-dim}
(`NCINE_BUILD_ASSET_PACKER`) @m_endspan; naming the target builds only the tool:

@code{.sh}
cmake -B ./build/ -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build ./build/ --target AssetPacker --parallel $(nproc)
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

The binary lands at @cpp "build/Utilities/AssetPacker/AssetPacker" @ce.

<b>2. Convert.</b> Pass the original game data and the repository's @cpp "Content" @ce separately:

@code{.sh}
./build/Utilities/AssetPacker/AssetPacker ./build/ConsoleContent \
    --source=<path to the original Jazz Jackrabbit 2> --content=./Content --target=console
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

The tree is written straight into the target directory @m_span{m-text m-dim} (not into a
@cpp "Content" @ce subdirectory; created if missing) @m_endspan. A full game installation, which has
both halves in @cpp "Source" @ce and @cpp "Content" @ce, can be passed as a single positional
argument instead @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp "AssetPacker <installation> ./build/ConsoleContent --target=console" @ce)
@m_endspan. A bare copy of the original game converted without @cpp "--content=" @ce produces a tree
with no fonts, @cpp "Metadata" @ce or translations that cannot even draw its menu; the tool warns
about it.

Pick the profile by the cinematics:

`--target=` | For | Cinematics
----------- | --- | ----------
`console` | Every console except the Dreamcast and the GameCube @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`wii` and `psp` are aliases) @m_endspan | Original `.j2v` files copied unchanged
`dreamcast` | @ref consoles-dreamcast "Sega Dreamcast" | Re-encoded into @ref Jazz2::VideoFormat @m_span{m-text m-dim} (LZ4 block per frame; the SH-4 cannot inflate the original in time) @m_endspan
`gamecube` | @ref consoles-gamecube "Nintendo GameCube" | Re-encoded the same way @m_span{m-text m-dim} (the original's interleaved streams cannot be read from disc fast enough) @m_endspan

@m_class{m-noindent}

Options that make the tree smaller @m_span{m-text m-dim} (the rest are in @ref asset-packer-usage)
@m_endspan:

-   `--originals-only` --- only the episodes the original game shipped
-   `--shareware-only` --- only the Shareware Demo
-   `--skip-non-episode-levels` --- drop levels that belong to no episode
-   `--video-downscale=N` --- shrink the cinematics by 2 to 4 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (re-encodes on any
    profile) @m_endspan

<b>3. Check the output.</b> A complete tree looks like this:

@code{.shell-session}
build/ConsoleContent/Prebaked.pak          # sprites and sounds, plus "Animations" and "Metadata"
build/ConsoleContent/Episodes/…            # the episodes and their levels
build/ConsoleContent/Tilesets/…
build/ConsoleContent/Music/…
build/ConsoleContent/Cinematics/intro.j2v  # and "ending.j2v"
build/ConsoleContent/Translations/…        # the ".mo" files
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

A tree with @cpp "Source.pak" @ce instead of @cpp "Prebaked.pak" @ce came from the `desktop`
profile and is wrong for a console.

<b>4. Point the console build at it</b> with `NCINE_CONTENT_DIR`:

@code{.sh}
cmake -B ./build/<console>/ … -D NCINE_CONTENT_DIR=$PWD/build/ConsoleContent
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

The variable is <b>cached</b>: give it on the first configuration of a build directory, or re-run CMake
with it on an existing one. Unset, it defaults to the repository's @cpp "Content" @ce, which boots to
a main menu with no episode. Each build stages the directory under the name @cpp "Content" @ce at
the place the console reads it:

Console | Content directory | Cache / save data
------- | ----------------- | -----------------
Nintendo 64 | @cpp "rom:/Content/" @ce @m_span{m-text m-dim} (ROM's DragonFS image) @m_endspan | @cpp "rom:/Cache/" @ce, read-only; preferences on the cartridge EEPROM @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp "eeprom:/JAZZ2CFG" @ce) @m_endspan
Dreamcast | @cpp "/cd/Content/" @ce @m_span{m-text m-dim} (disc image) @m_endspan | @cpp "/cd/Cache/" @ce, read-only; preferences and resumable state on a memory card @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp "/vmu/<port><unit>/Jazz2" @ce and @cpp "Jazz2.resume" @ce) @m_endspan
Wii | @cpp "sd:/apps/Jazz2/Content/" @ce | @cpp "sd:/apps/Jazz2/Cache/" @ce
GameCube | @cpp "dvd:/Content/" @ce @m_span{m-text m-dim} (disc image) @m_endspan, or @cpp "Jazz2/Content/" @ce on an SD card @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`sd:`, `carda:` or `cardb:`, see @ref consoles-gamecube-disc) @m_endspan | Disc: read-only, preferences and resumable state on a memory card @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp "mca:/Jazz2.config" @ce and @cpp "Jazz2.resume" @ce) @m_endspan; SD card: @cpp "Jazz2/Cache/" @ce, writable
Nintendo 3DS | @cpp "sdmc:/3ds/Jazz2/Content/" @ce | @cpp "sdmc:/3ds/Jazz2/Cache/" @ce
PlayStation Portable | @cpp "ms0:/PSP/GAME/Jazz2/Content/" @ce | @cpp "ms0:/PSP/GAME/Jazz2/Cache/" @ce
PlayStation 2 | @cpp "cdfs:/Content/" @ce @m_span{m-text m-dim} (disc image) @m_endspan | @cpp "cdfs:/Cache/" @ce, read-only, nothing staged
PlayStation 3 | @cpp "/app_home/Content/" @ce @m_span{m-text m-dim} (next to `EBOOT.BIN`) @m_endspan | @cpp "/dev_hdd0/game/JAZZ20000/USRDIR/Cache/" @ce, writable
PlayStation Vita | @cpp "app0:/Content/" @ce @m_span{m-text m-dim} (inside the VPK) @m_endspan | @cpp "ux0:/data/jazz2/Cache/" @ce
Switch | @cpp "romfs:/" @ce @m_span{m-text m-dim} (embedded in the `.nro`) @m_endspan | @cpp "sdmc:/Games/Jazz2/Cache/" @ce

@m_class{m-noindent}

On the PlayStation 3 @cpp "/app_home" @ce maps to the running executable's directory
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (`USRDIR`) @m_endspan, so the same build runs installed and straight from the
staged package directory, which is how RPCS3 boots it. The `Source`, configuration and log locations
of every platform are in @ref paths "File locations".

@cpp "Prebaked.pak" @ce marks a tree as prepared: a platform that could convert
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (Wii, 3DS, PSP, PS3, Vita, Switch) @m_endspan then skips the conversion, leaves
`Cache` alone and never looks for the original game files. To convert on the device instead, leave the prepared tree out and put an original
installation into the console's `Source` directory.

@m_class{m-noindent}

The @ref consoles-n64-content "Nintendo 64" packs the tree into the ROM's DragonFS image, the
@ref consoles-dreamcast-content "Dreamcast" and the @ref consoles-ps2-content "PlayStation 2" into a
disc image. For these three:

-   <b>The medium is all the game will ever have.</b> No conversion, no writable `Cache` and no
    content check --- an incomplete tree just shows a main menu with nothing to play.
-   <b>The staging directory is added to, not replaced.</b> `NCINE_CONTENT_DIR` is copied into its
    `Content` subdirectory on every build, so files removed from the tree survive and get packed into
    later images. The N64 build clears it first; for the Dreamcast and PS2 delete
    @cpp "<build dir>/cd/" @ce by hand after regenerating a tree.

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par The tool and the game must come from the same sources
A prepared tree is never rebuilt, and data from an older AssetPacker is not rejected with a message
--- it shows up as missing text, missing tilesets or a level that won't start. Regenerate the tree with
a freshly built tool after pulling data-format changes.
@par
Converted levels carry their own version after the file type byte @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see
@ref Jazz2::Formats::LevelFile) @m_endspan, and any other version is refused with a log line. A level that
can't be reconverted can be upgraded from the unversioned layout to version 1 by inserting a
@cpp u16 @ce `1` right after the file type byte.

@subsection consoles-common-features What is available on which console

Decided at configure time from the platform; values are for a default `Release` configuration.

Feature | N64 | Dreamcast | Wii | GameCube | 3DS | PSP | PS2 | PS3 | Vita | Switch
------- | --- | --------- | --- | -------- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---- | ------
Threads @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`NCINE_WITH_THREADS`) @m_endspan | **No** | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (pthread shim over libctru) @m_endspan | Yes | **No** | **No** | Yes | Yes
Asynchronous tracing @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`DEATH_TRACE_ASYNC`) @m_endspan | No | No | No | No | No | No | No | No | No | No
Audio backend | `N64` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (RSP mixer) @m_endspan | `AICA` | `ASND` | `ASND` | `NDSP` | `PSP` | `PS2` | `PS3` | `OpenAL` if the SDK has one | `OpenAL` if the SDK has one
Sound effects | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (needs DSP firmware, see @ref consoles-3ds-limits) @m_endspan | Yes | Yes | Yes | SDK-dependent | SDK-dependent
Module music | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (**XM64**, converted ahead, see @ref consoles-n64-limits) @m_endspan | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (libxmp, see @ref consoles-dreamcast-limits) @m_endspan | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (libxmp, see @ref consoles-wii-limits) @m_endspan | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (libxmp, see @ref consoles-wii-limits) @m_endspan | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (libxmp) @m_endspan | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (libxmp, see @ref consoles-psp-limits) @m_endspan | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (libxmp, see @ref consoles-ps2-limits) @m_endspan | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (libopenmpt, from source, single-threaded) @m_endspan | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (libopenmpt; libxmp packaged too, see @ref consoles-vita-limits) @m_endspan | SDK-dependent
Underwater low-pass filter | **No** | **No** | **No** | **No** | **No** | **No** | **No** | **No** | SDK-dependent | Yes
Local splitscreen | **No** --- needs threads | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (one controller) @m_endspan | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (one controller) @m_endspan | **No** --- needs threads | **No** --- needs threads | Yes | Yes
Online multiplayer | **No** | **No** | **No** | **No** | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (ENet only, see @ref consoles-3ds-limits) @m_endspan | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (ENet only, see @ref consoles-psp-limits) @m_endspan | **No** | **No** | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (ENet only, see @ref consoles-vita-limits) @m_endspan | Yes
Post-processing and rescale filters | **No** | **No** | **No** | **No** | **No** | **No** | **No** | Yes | Yes | Yes
X-ray and blur-behind tile layers @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`TILEMAP_LAYER_EFFECTS`) @m_endspan | **No** --- reduced at load | **No** --- reduced at load | **No** --- reduced at load | **No** --- reduced at load | **No** --- reduced at load | **No** --- reduced at load | **No** --- reduced at load | Yes @m_span{m-text m-dim} (microcode generated, not yet run on a console) @m_endspan | Yes | Yes
External `.shader` files | **No** | **No** | **No** | **No** | **No** | **No** | **No** | **No** @m_span{m-text m-dim} (no runtime compiler) @m_endspan | Yes | Yes
Converts the game data itself | **No** | **No** | Yes | **No** | Yes | Yes | **No** | Yes | Yes | Yes

@subsection consoles-common-audio Audio backends

`WITH_AUDIO` compiles the audio subsystem in, with exactly one <b>audio backend</b> chosen at
configure time from the platform. A backend implements @ref nCine::IAudioDevice; @ref nCine::AudioBuffer,
@ref nCine::IAudioPlayer and @ref nCine::AudioStream contain no backend calls, so a console only has
to provide a device. Backends live in @cpp "nCine/Audio/Backends/<backend>/" @ce.

Backend | `WITH_*` | What it drives
------- | -------- | --------------
@ref nCine::ALAudioDevice "AL" | `WITH_OPENAL` | System OpenAL on desktop, the SDK's own on the Switch and Vita
@ref nCine::AsndAudioDevice "ASND" | `WITH_ASND` | libogc's DSP mixer @m_span{m-text m-dim} (Wii, GameCube; ships with devkitPPC) @m_endspan
@ref nCine::AicaAudioDevice "AICA" | `WITH_AICA` | The Dreamcast sound processor through KallistiOS: wavetable channels for effects, `snd_stream` for music
@ref nCine::Ps3AudioDevice "PS3" | `WITH_PS3AUDIO` | PSL1GHT's libaudio; lv2 offers only an output ring, so this backend is a software mixer on the PPE
@ref nCine::PspAudioDevice "PSP" | `WITH_PSPAUDIO` | Integer software mixer into one `sceAudio` channel, on its own thread
@ref nCine::NdspAudioDevice "NDSP" | `WITH_NDSP` | Same mixer into one libctru NDSP channel on a higher-priority thread; the DSP resamples, so any mixing rate is valid. Needs the DSP firmware, see @ref consoles-3ds-limits
@ref nCine::N64AudioDevice "N64" | `WITH_N64AUDIO` | libdragon's RSP mixer into the AI ring. Effects stream from `.wav64` files the asset packer writes, music is libdragon's `.xm64` player, cinematic soundtracks play through libdragon's video player @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`NCINE_HAS_NATIVE_AUDIO`) @m_endspan
@ref nCine::Ps2AudioDevice "PS2" | `WITH_PS2AUDIO` | Software mixer into the `audsrv` IRX stream, topped up per frame @m_span{m-text m-dim} (no threads) @m_endspan; the SPU2 resamples in hardware
none | --- | Silent fallback when no backend is available or audio is turned off

Each console's SDK provides what its backend needs; nothing has to be installed by hand.
`NCINE_WITH_AUDIO` is @cpp ON @ce everywhere; clearing it removes the whole subsystem.

Backends that mix on the main thread starve when it blocks longer than they have queued, e.g. during a
level load. @ref nCine::IAudioDevice::beginBlockingOperation() and its counterpart are called around
the level load and the episode scan; the PS2 stops its stream for the duration
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref consoles-ps2-limits) @m_endspan.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par Notes for backend authors
-   <b>Dreamcast:</b> ALdc, the KallistiOS OpenAL from kos-ports, is unusable --- it panics the kernel
    at startup, ignores `AL_GAIN` and mishandles `alSourcei(AL_BUFFER, 0)`.
-   <b>PlayStation 3:</b> the ring is topped up once per frame from `updatePlayers()`
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (no threads) @m_endspan. The mix must be <b>clamped</b>, since out-of-range
    samples flip polarity. On a blocking operation the ring is zeroed so its last 85 ms don't repeat.
-   <b>PSP:</b> pspdev's OpenAL Soft 1.6 is too slow @m_span{m-text m-dim} (12--24% of the CPU)
    @m_endspan. The mixer runs on its output thread, so its lock must be a <b>kernel semaphore</b>, not
    a spin lock --- the higher-priority mixer would spin forever on a lock the main thread holds.
-   <b>Positional audio:</b> the software mixers share @ref nCine::AudioMixer::ComputeStereoGains().
    Sources arrive in physical units @m_span{m-text m-dim} (pixels times
    @ref nCine::IAudioDevice::LengthToPhysical, Y and Z negated) @m_endspan but the listener in raw
    pixels, so the listener must be scaled first, or sounds go silent away from the level origin.
-   <b>Streaming:</b> @ref nCine::AudioStream decodes one chunk ahead on a decoding thread and
    `enqueue()` <b>never waits</b> for it; a chunk that isn't ready is simply not queued. Without a
    decoding thread it decodes in place, unless the device takes over @m_span{m-text m-dim} (PS2, see
    @ref consoles-ps2-limits) @m_endspan. Level and menu music start two frames late
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`_musicStartDelay`) @m_endspan to clear the deferred-loading frames.
-   <b>Silent players</b> @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@ref nCine::IAudioPlayer::isSilent()) @m_endspan do
    no work: a stream decodes nothing but resumes where it left off, a buffer player never takes a
    source.
-   <b>Byte order:</b> @ref nCine::IAudioDevice buffer formats are native-endian. The WAVE and Vorbis
    readers byte-swap 16-bit samples on big-endian targets, and libxmp must be built with
    `WORDS_BIGENDIAN` there @m_span{m-text m-dim} (set from the target's byte order in
    `cmake/Findlibxmp.cmake`) @m_endspan, or 16-bit module samples play as loud noise on the Wii and
    GameCube.

@subsection consoles-common-logging Getting a log out of a console

Every console writes the same @ref Asserts.h "trace" output as desktop, but each needs a different
receiver. Set it up before debugging anything.

Console | Where the trace goes
------- | --------------------
Nintendo 64 | `stderr` via libdragon's debug sinks: the <b>ISViewer</b> window @m_span{m-text m-dim} (**ares** prints it to its terminal) @m_endspan and the <b>USB log</b> of an EverDrive/SC64, both probed at startup. The boot console shows startup messages until the renderer takes over
Dreamcast | `dbgio`: the <b>framebuffer console</b> during startup, then the <b>SCIF serial port</b>. Emulators print it to their log, `dc-tool` to its console
Wii / GameCube | A <b>USB Gecko</b> on EXI channel 1 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (memory-card slot B) @m_endspan, probed at startup; without one nothing is written. <b>Dolphin</b> serves it on TCP port 55020 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (or the next free one) @m_endspan with slot B set to "USB Gecko". `Jazz2.log` is written beside the content on an SD card, never to a GameCube memory card
Nintendo 3DS | `svcOutputDebugString`: <b>Azahar</b> logs it under `Debug.Emulated` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (hidden by its default filter, see @ref consoles-3ds-deploy) @m_endspan, or an attached debugger. The <b>bottom-screen console</b> shows every line until the renderer starts, then warnings and errors. `Jazz2.log` is written next to the `.3dsx`
PlayStation Portable | `stdout` via `sceIoWrite` on fd 1: <b>PPSSPP</b> log, `psplink` console, plus the <b>debug screen</b> until the GU takes over
PlayStation 2 | The EE <b>SIO transmit register</b> @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`0x1000F180`) @m_endspan: <b>PCSX2</b> shows it as EE console output with `EnableEEConsole` on, a serial cable on hardware. Plus the <b>boot console</b> @m_span{m-text m-dim} (libdebug) @m_endspan until the renderer starts --- after the disc wait, IOP module loads and content/memory-card probes --- so a stalled boot leaves its last line on screen
PlayStation 3 | `sysTtyWrite`: fd 1 and 2 go to the console TTY on hardware and to <b>RPCS3</b>'s log
PlayStation Vita | `sceClibPrintf`, read with host tools @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`psp2shell`, VitaCompanion) @m_endspan
Switch | `svcOutputDebugString`, read with a host debug logger; a log file on the SD card is always written too

@m_class{m-noindent}

`DEATH_TRACE_LOG_PATH` forces the trace into a file on platforms with writable storage
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (not the Dreamcast) @m_endspan. Verbose I/O lines are deferred and flushed only
when an error follows, so a sparse log is normal. Where `Jazz2.log` ends up on each console is listed
in @ref paths-consoles.

The log header names the device via @ref nCine::Application::GetDeviceHostname() --- the console
nickname (Wii, 3DS, Switch), WLAN MAC (PSP), OpenPSID (Vita, PS3), i.Link ID (PS2) or BIOS system ID
(Dreamcast); empty on the GameCube and N64. It also seeds the device ID sent to the servers.

@subsection consoles-common-indexedquads Tile layers are indexed meshes

A tile layer is drawn as one mesh @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`TILEMAP_USE_SINGLE_DRAW`, on everywhere
except the software renderer) @m_endspan, as are destructible debris and, with shaders, the lighting
quads. The mesh is <b>indexed</b>: four vertices per quad plus six indices from a shared pattern
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp RenderResources::GetQuadIndices() @ce --- `0, 1, 2, 0, 2, 3` per quad,
relative to the draw's first vertex, allocated once so a render queue may keep the pointer) @m_endspan.

-   <b>Shader backends</b> @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`GL`, `GXM`, `RSX`, `D3D11`, `Vulkan`) @m_endspan
    draw through the index buffer with base-vertex addressing; OpenGL|ES below 3.2 has no
    @cpp glDrawElementsBaseVertex() @ce and folds the base into the vertex format's offset
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp RenderCommand::Issue() @ce) @m_endspan.
-   <b>Fixed-function backends</b> @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`GU`, `GX`, `PVR`, `GS`, `RDP`, `PICA`,
    `LegacyGL`) @m_endspan resolve the indices on the CPU via @cpp ResolveHostIndices() @ce and a
    `vertexAt()` resolver; quad recognition @m_span{m-text m-dim} (one GE rectangle, `GX_QUADS` quad
    or GS sprite per tile) @m_endspan still works.

@cpp RenderResources::GetMaxQuadsPerDraw() @ce alone decides the chunk length, from the streaming
buffer sizes and the 16-bit index range @m_span{m-text m-dim} (sceGxm rejects indices above 63999)
@m_endspan; larger meshes are split on whole quads.

@subsection consoles-common-levelgrids What a level's grids cost

A level's <b>tile layers</b> and <b>event grid</b> are the largest allocations of a level load ---
dense, they would be over 3 MB for a large level @m_span{m-text m-dim} (768x64 tiles, up to eight
layers) @m_endspan. Two storage choices reduce that, transparently to readers:

-   <b>The event grid is sparse</b> on every platform: a @cpp uint16_t @ce slot per cell into a dense
    array of only the cells that have an event @m_span{m-text m-dim}
    (@ref Jazz2::Events::EventMap "EventMap" `::EventLayout`) @m_endspan. Empty cells read as a shared
    empty tile. Writes go through @cpp Edit() @ce, which allocates the cell; @cpp Find() @ce looks up
    without allocating. A reference from @cpp Edit() @ce is valid only until the next @cpp Edit() @ce.
-   <b>Draw-only layers store 4 bytes a tile</b> instead of 12 @m_span{m-text m-dim}
    (`LayerLayout::CompactDrawOnlyLayers`: N64, PSP, Dreamcast, 3DS, PS2) @m_endspan. @cpp Layout[i] @ce
    decodes into a small ring of scratch @ref Jazz2::Tiles::LayerTile "LayerTile" values, so a reference
    survives only the next few lookups; writes go through @cpp Set() @ce. Other platforms keep plain
    arrays.

Both grids are allocated in blocks of 4096 tiles and without throwing: a level whose grids don't fit
<b>fails to load</b> with the main menu's "Cannot load specified level!" instead of aborting.

@subsection consoles-common-skips Work the fixed-function consoles leave out

Shared optimizations on the CPU-transforming consoles; none changes the output visibly or audibly.

-   <b>A fully lit level draws no lightmap.</b> Backends that clamp the lightmap to darkening only
    declare `RHI_LIGHTING_DARKEN_ONLY` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp "nCine/Graphics/RHI/RhiFwd.h" @ce
    --- `PVR`, `GU`, `GS`, `GX`, `PICA`, `RDP`) @m_endspan, and
    @cpp CombineRenderer::PrepareSoftwareLighting() @ce then skips the lightmap entirely at full
    ambient level.
-   <b>A tile layer's visible window is cached between frames</b> with `TILEMAP_CACHE_LAYER_WINDOW`
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp "Jazz2/Tiles/TileMap.h" @ce) @m_endspan and re-issued under a new
    translation until it moves by a whole tile or a tile changes; animated tiles stay per-frame. It
    needs a backend whose tile-mesh dispatch applies the model matrix --- every shader backend plus
    `GX`, `PICA`, `GS` and `GU`. The N64 has its own cache in the RDP tile format; `PVR` and legacy GL
    walk every frame. Compact layers @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref consoles-common-levelgrids)
    @m_endspan carry a version stamp bumped on every write for this check.
-   <b>Slow libm calls are replaced.</b> libxmp's per-tick double `pow()`/`log()`/divisions and the
    fixed-function effects' `floorf()`/`ceilf()` are soft-float on these CPUs, so
    @cpp "nCine/Base/LibmOverrides.cpp" @ce takes them over through the linker's `--wrap`:

Console | Replaced | Why
------- | -------- | ---
Dreamcast | `pow`, `log`, `ceilf` | The SH-4 ABI defaults to single precision, so every double operation switches FPU mode
Nintendo 64 | `floorf`, `ceilf` | The VR4300 has no rounding instruction
PlayStation 2 | `pow`, `log`, double division, `floorf`, `ceilf` | Single-precision FPU only, and music decodes on the main thread
PSP | `pow`, `log`, double division, `floorf`, `ceilf` | Single-precision FPU only

@m_class{m-noindent}

`pow()` and `log()` are computed in single precision within a few ULPs; whole exponents go to newlib
exactly @m_span{m-text m-dim} (libxmp's Vorbis decoder needs exact integral powers) @m_endspan. Division
and rounding are bit-exact. A new target opts in by adding the file to its source list and the
`--wrap` options to its link, both in its arm of @cpp "cmake/ncine_extra_sources.cmake" @ce.

- - -

@section consoles-n64 Nintendo 64

A 93 MHz VR4300 with <b>8 MB</b> of RDRAM shared by CPU and graphics, and an RDP rasterizer whose
only texture memory is a <b>4 KB</b> TMEM. The <b>Expansion Pak is required</b> --- the base 4 MB
cannot hold the game. Renders through the `RDP` backend @m_span{m-text m-dim}
(@cpp "Sources/nCine/Graphics/RHI/RDP" @ce) @m_endspan on **libdragon** (rdpq), presented by the
`N64` window backend in 320x240 RGB565.

@subsection consoles-n64-toolchain Toolchain

libdragon ships a prebuilt `mips64-elf` GCC; only the library is built. Requirements the toolchain
file checks:

-   libdragon must come from the <b>preview</b> branch, and must <b>not</b> be built with `NDEBUG`
    (the log channels need symbols that build strips).
-   Pin a revision for reproducible builds (CI uses the one in @cpp ".github/workflows/n64.yml" @ce).
    It must be at least `7c57c409` (@cpp "rspq: make lowpri buffer handoff atomic" @ce) --- older
    revisions stop video with @cpp "rspq_next_buffer: wait loop timed out" @ce.

@code{.sh}
# Prebuilt cross-compiler (also available as .deb; extract anywhere, point N64_INST at it)
curl -LO https://github.com/DragonMinded/libdragon/releases/download/toolchain-continuous-prerelease/gcc-toolchain-mips64-x86_64.rpm
rpm2cpio gcc-toolchain-mips64-x86_64.rpm | cpio -idm
export N64_INST=$PWD/opt/libdragon
export PATH="$N64_INST/bin:$PATH"

# Library and host tools, from the preview branch
git clone -b preview https://github.com/DragonMinded/libdragon.git
make -C libdragon -j $(nproc) libdragon && make -C libdragon install
make -C libdragon -j $(nproc) tools && make -C libdragon tools-install

# zlib (required for compressed content, not packaged)
curl -L https://github.com/madler/zlib/releases/download/v1.3.1/zlib-1.3.1.tar.gz | tar xz
cd zlib-1.3.1
for f in adler32 compress crc32 deflate infback inffast inflate inftrees trees uncompr zutil; do
    mips64-elf-gcc -march=vr4300 -mtune=vr4300 -mabi=o64 -O2 -c $f.c; done
mips64-elf-gcc-ar rcs libz.a *.o
cp libz.a "$N64_INST/mips64-elf/lib/" && cp zlib.h zconf.h "$N64_INST/mips64-elf/include/"
@endcode

@subsection consoles-n64-content Preparing the content tree

This console gets its own conversion, `--target=n64`, which converts sound effects, music and
cinematics into libdragon's formats with libdragon's host tools --- that is what gives it music and
makes the game fit on a cartridge @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref asset-packer-n64) @m_endspan.
Everything else in @ref consoles-common-content applies.

@code{.sh}
./build/Utilities/AssetPacker/AssetPacker ./build/ConsoleContentN64 \
    --source=<path to the original Jazz Jackrabbit 2> --content=./Content \
    --target=n64 --n64-tools=$N64_INST
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

-   `audioconv64` is required; `videoconv64` plus **ffmpeg** and **ffprobe** encode the cinematics.
-   A packer built with **libopenmpt** pre-renders the tracks not in the game's own module format and
    mixes music into the cinematics' soundtracks.
-   Sprite sheets and tilesets are written in LZ4 (`NCINE_WITH_LZ4`, see @ref asset-packer-lz4).
-   A plain `console` tree also works, but has no music, and its cinematics must be re-encoded at half
    size (`recompress-video --video-downscale=2`) to play at a watchable rate.

@subsection consoles-n64-build Building

@code{.sh}
export N64_INST=<toolchain prefix>
cmake -B ./build/n64/ -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -D CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=./cmake/toolchains/n64.cmake \
    -D NCINE_CONTENT_DIR=<n64 content tree>
make -j $(nproc) -C ./build/n64/
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

The toolchain file is the project's own (libdragon has no CMake support) and reproduces the machine
flags of libdragon's `n64.mk`. LTO is force-disabled and `Release` uses `-O2`.

The build produces @cpp "build/n64/jazz2.elf" @ce and the bootable @cpp "build/n64/jazz2.z64" @ce.
A POST_BUILD step reproduces libdragon's ROM layout:

-   `n64sym` (symbol table for on-console backtraces), `n64elfcompress` (stripped, compressed
    executable) and `mkdfs` (**DragonFS** image of the content), concatenated by `n64tool`;
-   `ed64romconfig` sets **eeprom16k** (2 KB EEPROM for preferences) and a **Rumble Pak** in
    controller 1. The real-time clock is deliberately not declared --- an EverDrive cannot provide it
    together with EEPROM.
-   Content is staged by @cpp "cmake/n64_stage_content.cmake" @ce, which drops what the console cannot
    play (original music formats, the original of a cinematic that has a video).

@subsection consoles-n64-deploy Deploying and running

-   <b>Emulator</b> --- @cpp "jazz2.z64" @ce boots in **ares** or **simple64** (both default to an
    Expansion Pak). ares prints the trace (ISViewer) to its terminal.
-   <b>Real hardware</b> --- copy the ROM to an **EverDrive-64** or **SC64**; the save type is read from
    the header. An Expansion Pak must be installed.
-   <b>New content without the toolchain</b> ---
    `AssetPacker swap-content jazz2.z64 --content=<n64 content tree>`
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref asset-packer-swap-content) @m_endspan.

@subsection consoles-n64-limits Limits and known issues

This port is <b>experimental</b>: the CPU is an order of magnitude below the next-slowest console, and
a level frame takes about 14--31 ms in ares depending on the level.

<b>Memory and size</b>

-   <b>8 MB unified RDRAM</b>, about 6.3 MB of it heap. Strictest budgets of any console: no episode
    backgrounds, half the debris caps, the outgoing level is released before the next loads, and the
    lightmap is combined at 1/12 of the output resolution (27x20 texels).
-   <b>Fragmentation is what fails first</b>, so nothing large is allocated as one block: level grids
    come in 4096-tile blocks (sparse/compact forms, see
    @ref consoles-common-levelgrids "the section above"), the tileset atlas is built and uploaded in
    chunks of at most 256 KB, and direct-colour textures are converted straight into their RDP store.
    A level that does not fit returns to the menu with a message. All animations are deferred (sheets
    read on first use); the few that would hitch are read behind the loading screen.
-   <b>The executable (1.9 MB) comes out of the same 8 MB as the heap.</b> To keep it small, the bundled
    JSON library formats with @cpp fputs() @ce and parses with @cpp strtod() @ce (string streams pull
    in ~350 KB of @cpp <locale> @ce), the port defines its own @cpp std::terminate @ce handler
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp "nCine/MainApplication.cpp" @ce, avoids linking the demangler)
    @m_endspan, and the game target is built without RTTI on every platform, since casts use
    @cpp runtime_cast<T>() @ce (`DEATH_USE_RUNTIME_CAST`; turning it off brings RTTI back). The last
    two also help the @ref consoles-dreamcast "Dreamcast" and @ref consoles-ps2 "PlayStation 2".
-   The display is double buffered (not triple), and the tileset atlas has no
    `TileSet::TilePadding` border (75--84 KB saved). That is valid only because tile layers are drawn
    1:1 with point sampling --- any platform that scales or bilinearly samples them needs the border.
-   <b>The ROM must fit in 64 MB</b> (flashcart and PI bus limit); the build fails if it does not. A
    `--target=n64` tree is about 39 MB with all music and both cinematics.
-   <b>Preferences live in cartridge EEPROM</b> via eepromfs (@cpp "eeprom:/JAZZ2CFG" @ce, 2 KB). An
    emulator or flashcart that ignores the header's save type leaves the game unable to save (warned
    at startup, not an error).

<b>Audio and video</b>

-   <b>Music plays from converted modules</b>: libopenmpt and libxmp need 0.5--4 MB per module, so the
    packer translates the `.j2b` modules into libdragon's `.xm64` (only patterns in memory,
    VADPCM instruments streamed from the cartridge) and pre-renders the three tracks in other formats.
-   Audio costs about 3 ms CPU per frame. <b>Every streamed channel must be VADPCM</b> --- a single raw
    PCM stream limits every mixer round to ~120 samples and roughly doubles the cost. VADPCM's Huffman
    stage is left off (the CPU would have to undo it).
-   <b>A paused sound effect restarts from the beginning</b> when resumed: the mixer has no paused
    state and effects have no VADPCM seek points; libdragon asserts ("invalid VADPCM seeking point")
    on any other position.
-   <b>Cinematics are MPEG-1</b>, played by libdragon's player (CPU + RSP), which owns the main thread
    while playing. The port tops audio up once more per frame and holds a frame back while the
    soundtrack lags, otherwise sound runs at ~0.8x @m_span{m-text m-dim}
    (@cpp "nCine/Backends/N64/N64FullMotionVideo.cpp" @ce) @m_endspan.

<b>Hardware quirks</b>

-   <b>FPU exception traps are masked at startup.</b> A non-`NDEBUG` libdragon enables overflow,
    divide-by-zero and invalid-operation traps, but the game assumes IEEE behaviour (e.g.
    @cpp FLT_MAX @ce squared), so `FCR31` enable bits are cleared, keeping only flush-to-zero.
-   <b>Video filters:</b> the display uses `FILTERS_RESAMPLE` (no AA, no de-dither) and the RDP writes
    undithered pixels (`SOM_RGBDITHER_NONE`). De-dither needs more than 320 px width, and
    `VI_AA_MODE_NONE` hits a documented NTSC hardware bug at this width --- don't switch.
-   <b>TMEM is per-primitive</b>: 4 KB (2 KB for paletted texels) uploaded before each draw, so frame
    cost tracks distinct texture windows. Tiles upload one 32x32 tile at a time (same-tile runs
    coalesced); sprites larger than a window are drawn as row bands. Windows alternate between
    TILE0 and TILE1.
    -   Unused tile descriptors are given harmless values at startup --- a real console powers up with
        random contents (emulators zero them), which two-cycle draws (colorized text) would sample.
    -   Two-cycle draws skip alpha compare on screen (the hardware is a pixel off) and keep it only in
        render targets.
    -   A tile's TMEM window must be derived from the same fixed-point texel coordinates as its
        rectangle; from floats, non-power-of-two atlases overflow the 1 KB slot.
-   <b>Additive blending is drawn as alpha-over</b>: the RDP's additive mode wraps past white to black.
    Effects that need true summing accumulate on the CPU and are unaffected.
-   <b>A hung RDP reports itself on screen.</b> A watchdog on the VI interrupt detects the RDP stuck on
    one command for 100 ms, shows its registers, the last state commands and the command words around
    the stop, and halts. Photograph it --- emulators do not reproduce these hangs, and a flashcart
    without USB cannot deliver libdragon's log.
-   <b>The classic main menu background uses different geometry</b> (texel coordinates are 10.5
    fixed point, the large tile exceeds TMEM): it animates correctly but not pixel-identically, with
    zoom narrowed to 0.75 +- 0.05 (original 0.6 +- 0.2) for a steady 40 ms frame.

<b>Rendering fast paths (N64-only)</b>

-   The frame is CPU-bound; the RDP is never waited on.
-   Each tile layer has a <b>cached mesh</b> of its visible window, rebuilt only when the window moves
    by a whole tile; animated tiles are kept out and resolved per frame. Records wholly outside the
    scissor are neither uploaded nor drawn.
-   Tiles reach the RDP as one 16-byte integer record each (@cpp RHI::RDP::TileRecord @ce, drawn as
    @cpp PrimitiveType::Points @ce, a form only this backend gets). Debris keeps the indexed
    four-corner form (rotated quads).
-   Sprites whose window fits TMEM are rounded to the fixed-point grid once and drawn from integers
    (@cpp SubmitSpriteRect() @ce), including the HUD icons. A point-sampled mirrored sprite starts
    1/32 texel inside its far edge, as mirrored tiles do.
-   The plain sprite effect is recognized at link time (@cpp RdpShaderProgram::DispatchFacts::PlainSprite @ce),
    and axis-aligned textured instances go straight to a textured rectangle; rotated or untextured
    ones take the general path.
-   <b>Text is drawn without shadows</b> (@cpp Font::ShadowsEnabled @ce is off); sprite shadows under
    the HUD icons and the logo stay.
-   Glyphs are written straight into batched commands (@cpp RenderBatcher::BeginDirectBatch() @ce).
-   When profiling, compare release builds --- code layout alone moves timings by milliseconds.

- - -

@section consoles-dreamcast Sega Dreamcast

A 200 MHz SH-4 with <b>16 MB</b> main memory, <b>8 MB</b> video memory and a PowerVR2 (CLX2): no
programmable shading, no hardware scissor, 16-bit texture formats only. Renders through the `PVR`
backend @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp "Sources/nCine/Graphics/RHI/PVR" @ce) @m_endspan on
**KallistiOS**, presented by the `Dc` window backend in 640x480 RGB565.

@subsection consoles-dreamcast-toolchain Toolchain

KallistiOS and its `sh-elf` cross-compiler are built from source; there is no prebuilt package.

@code{.sh}
git clone https://github.com/KallistiOS/KallistiOS.git kos
cd kos

# Build the sh-elf (SH-4) cross-compiler
make -C utils/kos-chain

# The sample configuration matches the paths the toolchain just used
cp doc/environ.sh.sample environ.sh
source environ.sh
make -j $(nproc)

# zlib (required for compressed content) from kos-ports
git clone https://github.com/KallistiOS/kos-ports.git ../kos-ports
make -C ../kos-ports/zlib install
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

For a non-default install prefix, edit @cpp "utils/kos-chain/Makefile.cfg" @ce first, then
@cpp "environ.sh" @ce so `KOS_BASE`, `KOS_CC_BASE` and `KOS_PORTS` point at the built tree.

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par Source environ.sh before CMake, in every terminal
The KOS toolchain file finds the compiler, linker script and ports through its variables and fails
without them. `$KOS_CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN` points at the toolchain file (the `kos-cmake` wrapper in
@cpp "utils/build_wrappers" @ce passes it automatically). Use `kallistios.toolchain.cmake`;
`dreamcast.toolchain.cmake` is a deprecated alias.

@m_class{m-noindent}

For a bootable disc image, **mkdcdisc** must be on `PATH`:

@code{.sh}
git clone https://gitlab.com/simulant/mkdcdisc.git
cd mkdcdisc
meson setup build && ninja -C build
sudo cp ./build/mkdcdisc /usr/local/bin/
@endcode

@subsection consoles-dreamcast-content Preparing the content tree

The disc is the only medium: no first-run conversion, no writable `Cache`, and the <b>build</b>
authors the content into the image --- so the tree must exist before building. Prepare it as in
@ref consoles-common-content, but with the `dreamcast` profile:

@code{.sh}
# 1. The tool (any desktop build directory already has it)
cmake -B ./build/ -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build ./build/ --target AssetPacker --parallel $(nproc)

# 2. Convert with the Dreamcast profile - NOT "--target=console"
./build/Utilities/AssetPacker/AssetPacker ./build/DreamcastContent \
    --source=<path to the original Jazz Jackrabbit 2> --content=./Content --target=dreamcast

# 3. Check that Prebaked.pak exists
ls ./build/DreamcastContent
@endcode

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par Use `dreamcast`, not `console`
Both produce the same layout, but `console` keeps the original `.j2v` cinematics, which cost the SH-4
55--115 ms per frame (budget 42 ms), so the intro runs at a few frames a second. `dreamcast`
re-encodes them into @ref Jazz2::VideoFormat (under 1 ms per frame, larger files)
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref asset-packer-cinematics-format) @m_endspan.

@m_class{m-noindent}

-   <b>The tree must contain `"Prebaked.pak"`.</b> The Dreamcast packaging does not rename a
    @cpp "Source.pak" @ce, so a `desktop` cache produces a disc that loads nothing and shows a main
    menu with no episodes. Check for the file, not the directory.
-   <b>Trim before authoring</b> (`--originals-only`, `--skip-non-episode-levels`,
    `--video-downscale=N`) --- the image cannot be changed afterwards. Keep `Music`: this console
    plays it @m_span{m-text m-dim} (via libxmp, see @ref consoles-dreamcast-limits) @m_endspan.

@subsection consoles-dreamcast-build Building

@code{.sh}
source <path to KOS>/environ.sh
cmake -B ./build/dreamcast/ -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -D CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=${KOS_BASE}/utils/cmake/kallistios.toolchain.cmake \
    -D NCINE_CONTENT_DIR=$PWD/build/DreamcastContent
make -j $(nproc) -C ./build/dreamcast/
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

-   `NCINE_CONTENT_DIR` is cached: pass it on the <b>first</b> configure (or re-run CMake with it).
    Without it the repository's own @cpp "Content" @ce is used, which has no episode to play.
-   LTO is force-disabled (it makes `sh-elf` GCC 15.2 abort), `Release` uses `-O2`, and
    `DEATH_USE_FAST_MATH` is never applied on this platform.

The build produces @cpp "build/dreamcast/jazz2.elf" @ce and, if `mkdcdisc` was found,
@cpp "build/dreamcast/jazz2.cdi" @ce with the content at @cpp "/cd/Content" @ce. The POST_BUILD step:

@code{.sh}
# 1. Stage the tree under a directory literally named "Content"
cmake -E copy_directory <NCINE_CONTENT_DIR> ./build/dreamcast/cd/Content
# 2. Author the image from the ELF and that directory
mkdcdisc -e ./build/dreamcast/jazz2.elf -d ./build/dreamcast/cd/Content \
    -n "Jazz2 Resurrection" -o ./build/dreamcast/jazz2.cdi
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

`mkdcdisc` puts a directory on the disc under its own name and the game looks for
@cpp "/cd/Content/" @ce --- a hand-written `mkdcdisc` call on @cpp "build/DreamcastContent" @ce
produces a disc the game cannot read.

@m_class{m-block m-danger}

@par The staging directory is never cleared
@cpp "cmake -E copy_directory" @ce keeps files no longer in the source, so anything ever staged into
@cpp "build/dreamcast/cd/" @ce ends up in every later image (dropped levels, renamed packages, a stale
`Source.pak`). After regenerating the content tree, run `rm -rf ./build/dreamcast/cd/` before
building. (The Nintendo 64 build clears its staging directory itself; this one does not.)

@m_class{m-block m-danger}

@par Install mkdcdisc before configuring
The disc image step is added only if `find_program(mkdcdisc)` succeeds <b>at configure time</b>, and
the result is cached. If installed later, re-run CMake with `-U MKDCDISC_EXECUTABLE` or delete the
build directory. Without it, configure prints `mkdcdisc not found, bootable CDI image will not be
created` and only the ELF is built.

@subsection consoles-dreamcast-swap Changing the content of a finished image

No KallistiOS toolchain or `mkdcdisc` is needed to replace the content:

@code{.sh}
./build/Utilities/AssetPacker/AssetPacker swap-content ./build/dreamcast/jazz2.cdi \
    --content=./build/DreamcastContent
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

`swap-content` keeps the bootstrap and executable as they are (`1ST_READ.BIN` stays scrambled) and
rewrites the image around a new `Content` from a `--target=dreamcast` conversion. It runs wherever
the packer does, and the image keeps its size if the new content fits (usually). The `--content=`
directory becomes `Content` on the disc whatever its name, and since the image is rebuilt rather
than patched, the staging-directory trap above does not apply. See @ref asset-packer-swap-content;
the @ref consoles-ps2-swap "PlayStation 2 image" uses the same command.

@subsection consoles-dreamcast-deploy Deploying and running

-   <b>Emulator</b> --- @cpp "jazz2.cdi" @ce boots in **Flycast**, **lxdream** or **redream**.
-   <b>Real hardware</b> --- burn the CDI with a tool that handles the Dreamcast multi-session layout,
    or use an optical drive emulator such as GDEMU/MODE.
-   <b>`dc-tool`</b> over serial or a Broadband Adapter uploads @cpp "jazz2.elf" @ce for fast
    iteration, but the content is still read from @cpp "/cd/Content/" @ce, so a disc must be present.

<b>Logs in Flycast:</b> set `Debug.SerialConsoleEnabled = yes` in @cpp "flycast/emu.cfg" @ce while
Flycast is <b>not</b> running (it rewrites the file on exit and can revert the setting, which looks
like a crash before `main()`). Startup messages go to the on-screen framebuffer console, not serial,
and serial output is block-buffered, so a crash loses everything since the last flush.

@subsection consoles-dreamcast-limits Limits and known issues

<b>Display and memory</b>

-   <b>The logical view is 640x480</b> (not the 720x405 of 16:9 platforms), so it maps 1:1 onto the
    4:3 480-line output. It is ~1.4x the pixels; lower <em>Rendering Resolution</em> in
    Options > Graphics if frame rate matters more
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @cpp Jazz2::Rendering::UpscaleRenderPass::DefaultViewWidth @ce) @m_endspan.
-   <b>Main memory is the binding constraint</b>: the heap is roughly <b>13.8 MB</b>. Exhaustion shows
    in the log as `Out of memory. Requested sbrk_base …`, then `std::bad_alloc` and an abort (the
    emulator reports only a CPU exception). Video memory reports `Out of PVR memory allocating …`.
-   Console-only budgets: debris is capped (bursts coarsened, not truncated), the outgoing level is
    released before the next loads, and PVR texture host copies are RLE-compressed. The
    <b>"Particle Quality"</b> option in Options > Graphics (@ref Jazz2::ParticleQuality, all
    platforms) reduces further: <em>Low</em> halves bursts and weather, <em>Off</em> drops debris and
    weather, <em>High</em> is unrestricted.
-   After every level load the log has a line
    `Memory (level loaded): heap … VRAM … sound RAM …` (see @ref nCine::Backends::DcPlatform).

<b>Audio</b>

-   Sound effects live in the AICA's own <b>2 MB sound RAM</b>, not on the heap. Main memory holds the
    module decoder, three 16 KB buffers per stream, and sounds too long for a channel.
-   <b>Music plays through libxmp</b> (`NCINE_WITH_XMP`, on by default): a loaded module costs
    0.5--4 MB versus libopenmpt's 4.6--12.5 MB, and with libopenmpt the larger levels run out of
    memory. `-D NCINE_WITH_XMP=OFF` brings libopenmpt back (both are compiled from source). libxmp
    cannot read the four `.mo3` tracks.
-   Sound effects use AICA wavetable channels (one per mono channel; stereo samples are
    de-interleaved on upload), see @ref nCine::AicaAudioDevice. When one has finished is <b>predicted
    from length and rate</b> --- real hardware never clears the key-on bit of a finished one-shot
    (emulators do). Music uses KallistiOS's `snd_stream`, of which there are only <b>four</b>.
-   A channel addresses at most <b>65534 samples</b> (~3 s at 22 kHz). A longer sound that fits after
    one halving of its sample rate is halved (logged by @ref nCine::AicaAudioDevice::uploadBuffer);
    one that would need more is kept in main memory and played through a `snd_stream` handle (shared
    with music, no pitch changes). Only if that memory is unavailable is it halved further.
-   <b>8-bit samples are converted to signed on upload</b>; uploaded unsigned they crackle loudly
    (nearly all original sounds are 8-bit).
-   Panning and attenuation are computed on the CPU; the per-channel low-pass filter is not reachable
    through KallistiOS, so nothing is muffled underwater.
-   Module music renders at 22050 Hz (the AICA resamples for free) and uses single-precision
    `pow()`/`log()` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref consoles-common-skips) @m_endspan.
-   A stream's rate is fixed once started, so pitch changes apply to sound effects only.
-   <b>Streams are stopped during a load</b> and resumed afterwards, so loads are silent rather than
    looping the last fragment (see @ref nCine::IAudioDevice::beginBlockingOperation).

<b>Rendering</b>

-   Textures are 16-bit (ARGB4444, or palettized), clipping is geometric (no hardware scissor), and
    there is no post-processing tier: no rescale filters, the lightmap is combined on the CPU.
-   A scissor covering the whole viewport is ignored (it would clip nothing); splitscreen and partial
    menu scissors still clip, and primitives wholly inside a narrower scissor skip clipping.
-   The lightmap's ARGB4444 texture persists; only the lit spans of this and the previous frame are
    converted (`RHI_LIGHTING_LIT_SPANS`) while size and ambient stay unchanged.

<b>Saving</b>

-   Preferences and resumable state go to the first VMU @m_span{m-text m-dim}
    (@cpp "/vmu/a1/Jazz2" @ce and @cpp "/vmu/a1/Jazz2.resume" @ce) @m_endspan. With no card there is
    nowhere to save (warned at startup, not an error). The level cache is read from the disc.
-   Both files carry a <b>VMS header</b> (descriptions + 32x32 icon) so the file manager lists them;
    it costs two extra blocks per file
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref Jazz2::PreferencesCache::DescribeNextMemoryCardFile) @m_endspan.
    The icon is @cpp "Sources/Icons/Dreamcast/Icon.ico" @ce (32x32, 4 bpp, 15 colours + transparency,
    not animated), converted at configure time by @cpp "cmake/ncine_dreamcast_icon.cmake" @ce with no
    host image tool --- replace that file to change it.
-   Online multiplayer is disabled; local splitscreen works.

- - -

@section consoles-wii Nintendo Wii

A 729 MHz PowerPC (Broadway) with 24 MB MEM1 and 64 MB MEM2, and the fixed-function Hollywood GPU
(16-stage TEV). Renders through the `GX` backend @m_span{m-text m-dim}
(@cpp "Sources/nCine/Graphics/RHI/GX" @ce) @m_endspan on **libogc**, presented by the `Ogc` window
backend in the console's preferred video mode (PAL 50 Hz or NTSC/EDTV 60 Hz, interlaced or
progressive).

@subsection consoles-wii-toolchain Toolchain

@code{.sh}
# Install devkitPro's pacman, then the Wii group and zlib
sudo dkp-pacman -S --needed wii-dev ppc-zlib

export DEVKITPRO=/opt/devkitpro
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

`ppc-zlib` is <b>not</b> part of `wii-dev` but is required. The toolchain file is
@cpp "$DEVKITPRO/cmake/Wii.cmake" @ce.

@subsection consoles-wii-build Building

@code{.sh}
cmake -B ./build/wii/ -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -D CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=${DEVKITPRO}/cmake/Wii.cmake \
    -D NCINE_CONTENT_DIR=$PWD/build/ConsoleContent
make -j $(nproc) -C ./build/wii/
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

Besides @cpp "build/wii/jazz2.dol" @ce the build stages the Homebrew Channel SD layout:

@code{.shell-session}
build/wii/sd/apps/Jazz2/boot.dol
build/wii/sd/apps/Jazz2/Content/…
@endcode

@subsection consoles-wii-deploy Deploying and running

-   <b>Real hardware</b> --- copy @cpp "build/wii/sd/" @ce to the root of an SD card or USB drive and
    launch from the <b>Homebrew Channel</b>. @cpp "apps/Jazz2/meta.xml" @ce is optional.
-   <b>Dolphin</b> --- make a FAT32 image from the staging tree and enable the virtual SD card:

@code{.sh}
truncate -s 512M sd.raw && mkfs.vfat -F 32 sd.raw
mcopy -i sd.raw -s ./build/wii/sd/apps ::/
# Dolphin.ini: [Core] WiiSDCard = True, WiiSDCardPath = <path to sd.raw>
flatpak run org.DolphinEmu.dolphin-emu -e ./build/wii/jazz2.dol -b
@endcode

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par An SD card is required, even in an emulator
Without one the game stops at <em>Cannot access the SD card</em>. Dolphin can also sync a host
folder instead of an image (`WiiSDCardEnableFolderSync`).

@m_class{m-noindent}

<b>Logs:</b> put a **USB Gecko** in memory-card slot B (`SlotB = 7` in `Dolphin.ini`) and read
Dolphin's TCP socket on port <b>55020</b>; without the adapter nothing is written.
<b>Frame dumps:</b> `DumpXFBTarget = True` in @cpp "GFX.ini" @ce writes every displayed frame as PNG.

@subsection consoles-wii-limits Limits and known issues

-   <b>The logical view is 640x480 on a 4:3 set</b> (not 720x405), ~1.4x the pixels; lower
    <em>Rendering Resolution</em> in Options > Graphics if needed. With widescreen set in the system
    settings, the view is fitted to 16:9 (640x360) and the TV stretches it
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp nCine::Backends::OgcGfxDevice::displayAspect() @ce, reads
    `CONF_GetAspectRatio()`) @m_endspan.
-   <b>Music plays through libxmp</b> (`NCINE_WITH_XMP`, on by default): 0.5--4 MB per module versus
    libopenmpt's 4.6--12.5 MB, and libopenmpt would also be compiled into the binary (not packaged by
    devkitPro). libxmp cannot read the four `.mo3` tracks; `-D NCINE_WITH_XMP=OFF` switches back.
-   <b>Sound uses libogc's ASND</b>, not OpenAL: the DSP mixes up to <b>16 voices</b> at 48 kHz, which
    is also the engine's source limit (64 on desktop). Panning and attenuation are folded into the
    per-voice volume on the CPU (@cpp AsndAudioDevice::computeVolume() @ce).
-   ASND holds only two buffers per voice, so the stream queue lives in the backend
    (@ref nCine::AsndAudioDevice::updatePlayers).
-   The DSP reads samples from main memory by DMA (32-byte aligned and padded buffers); there is no
    separate sound RAM, so sound effects share the heap.
-   No DSP filter stage, so nothing is muffled underwater.
-   Online multiplayer is disabled; local splitscreen works.
-   No post-processing tier: no rescale filters; the lightmap is combined on the CPU (skipped in fully
    lit levels, see @ref consoles-common-skips), converted in one pass into the 4x4-tiled RGBA8 GPU
    layout, using `frsqrte` + two Newton steps since the CPU has no square-root instruction.
-   Textures are converted to GX formats (CI8 with hardware TLUT for palettized ones); effect passes
    become TEV stages generated from the `.shader` files.
-   Palette data is byte-swapped on load (big-endian) --- keep in mind when comparing palette dumps with
    desktop.

- - -

@section consoles-gamecube Nintendo GameCube

Same code path as the Wii (`GX` + `Ogc` backends) on a 486 MHz Gekko and the Flipper GPU, but with
<b>24 MB of RAM and no MEM2</b>. Runs from its own disc, or from an SD card in an SD Gecko
(memory-card slot) or SD2SP2 (serial port).

@subsection consoles-gamecube-toolchain Toolchain

@code{.sh}
sudo dkp-pacman -S --needed gamecube-dev ppc-zlib

export DEVKITPRO=/opt/devkitpro
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

`ppc-zlib` is not part of `gamecube-dev`. The toolchain file is
@cpp "$DEVKITPRO/cmake/GameCube.cmake" @ce. The disc image is made by
@ref asset-packer "AssetPacker", so a host build of it is needed too (any desktop build has one).

@subsection consoles-gamecube-build Building

@code{.sh}
cmake -B ./build/gamecube/ -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -D CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=${DEVKITPRO}/cmake/GameCube.cmake \
    -D NCINE_CONTENT_DIR=$PWD/build/ConsoleContentGameCube \
    -D GAMECUBE_ASSETPACKER_EXECUTABLE=$PWD/build/Utilities/AssetPacker/AssetPacker
make -j $(nproc) -C ./build/gamecube/
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

Prepare the tree with `--target=gamecube` (LZ4 sprites/tilesets, see @ref asset-packer-lz4, and
cinematics re-encoded into @ref Jazz2::VideoFormat); a `console` tree works but its intro falls far
behind the music. The build stages the content both as a disc image and as an SD card layout:

@code{.shell-session}
build/gamecube/jazz2.iso                 # bootable disc image
build/gamecube/sd/Jazz2/Jazz2.dol
build/gamecube/sd/Jazz2/Content/…
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

Without `GAMECUBE_ASSETPACKER_EXECUTABLE` (or `AssetPacker` on `PATH`) only the SD layout is staged.
The staged `Content` is cleared before each copy.

@subsection consoles-gamecube-disc The disc image

`AssetPacker create-image` makes the image (see @ref asset-packer-gamecube): an ISO 9660 volume with
the executable and `Content`, plus the disc header and an <b>apploader</b> in the system area, built
from @cpp "Sources/nCine/Backends/Ogc/GameCubeApploader.c" @ce (none ships with the toolchain). Game
ID `GJJEJR` (`GAMECUBE_GAME_ID`; no retail game, so Dolphin applies no game-specific settings),
region NTSC-U. `AssetPacker swap-content` (also in its web version) replaces the content of a
finished image without the toolchain.

The disc is read through the game's own driver (`OgcDvd`, mounted as `dvd:`; libogc's ISO 9660
driver leaks and resets the drive). Content is searched in this order
(`ContentResolver::InitializePaths()`):

1.  `Content` <b>next to the executable</b> (when a loader started it from SD).
2.  <b>The disc</b>, if it is this game's and the drive is ready --- so disc content wins over a stale
    tree on a card.
3.  `Jazz2/Content` on each <b>SD card</b>: `sd:` (SD2SP2), then `carda:` and `cardb:` (SD Gecko).
4.  The disc again, resetting and spinning up the drive.

If none is found the boot console says so and stops. With SD content, settings, resumable state,
cache and log go to the card. With disc content, saves go to the first usable <b>memory card</b>
(slot A, then B): `Jazz2.config` and `Jazz2.resume`, a few 8 KB blocks each, with icon and comment
(see `OgcMemoryCard.h`). With no memory card nothing is saved.

@subsection consoles-gamecube-deploy Deploying and running

-   <b>Dolphin</b> --- boot @cpp "build/gamecube/jazz2.iso" @ce (Dolphin has no GameCube SD adapter, so
    the image is the only way). Slot A holds a memory card by default ("GCI Folder" saves `.gci` files
    under @cpp "GC/USA/Card A/" @ce); keep slot B on "USB Gecko" for the log (trace goes to EXI
    channel 1 = slot B).
-   <b>Real hardware, SD card</b> --- copy @cpp "build/gamecube/sd/" @ce to a card in an <b>SD2SP2</b> or
    <b>SD Gecko</b> (either slot) and start @cpp "Jazz2/Jazz2.dol" @ce from <b>Swiss</b>.
-   <b>Real hardware, disc image</b> --- an optical drive emulator (GCLoader, CubeODE, FlippyDrive) or a
    burned disc on a console that boots them. Untested: only known to boot in Dolphin. Don't load the
    image from a card via Swiss (its disc-read patching does not fit this game); use the SD layout.

@subsection consoles-gamecube-limits Limits and known issues

Everything in @ref consoles-wii-limits applies, plus:

-   <b>Memory is much tighter</b>: 24 MB, no second pool. The Dreamcast's console budgets apply, ASND
    keeps every decoded sound effect in main memory, and the episode list draws no backdrops (over
    5 MB).
-   No Bluetooth: only GameCube controllers (the Wii build also links `wiiuse` and `bte` for Wii
    Remotes).
-   Dolphin's default DSP HLE sometimes raises ASND's DSP interrupt without its mail, which would hang
    libogc's handler forever; the ASND backend lets such an interrupt pass (`AsndAudioDevice.cpp`).
    No effect on real hardware.

- - -

@section consoles-3ds Nintendo 3DS

A 268 MHz ARM11 MPCore @m_span{m-text m-dim} (804 MHz with the L2 cache on a New 3DS, requested at
startup) @m_endspan with 64 MB of application memory @m_span{m-text m-dim} (more on a New 3DS; the
first log line reports how libctru split it between the heap and the linear heap) @m_endspan and a DMP
PICA200 driven through <b>citro3d</b>. The port renders through the `PICA` backend
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp "Sources/nCine/Graphics/RHI/PICA" @ce) @m_endspan, presented by the `Ctr`
window backend on the top screen at 400x240. The bottom screen is a text console: startup messages until
the renderer takes over, warnings and errors afterwards.

The only programmable stage is a passthrough vertex shader @m_span{m-text m-dim}
(@cpp "Sources/Shaders/Pica/Sprite.v.pica" @ce, assembled by <b>picasso</b>) @m_endspan. Quads are
transformed on the CPU, effects come from ShaderCompiler's `pica` fixed-function tables
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref shader-compiler-fixed-function) @m_endspan as texture-combiner
programs, and lighting is the software lightmap.

@subsection consoles-3ds-toolchain Toolchain

From devkitPro's pacman repository: the `3ds-dev` group @m_span{m-text m-dim} (devkitARM, libctru,
citro3d, `3ds-cmake`, and the `3dstools`, `picasso` and `bin2s` packaging uses) @m_endspan, `3ds-zlib`
for the content, and `3ds-curl` + `3ds-mbedtls` for the update check and the online server list:

@code{.sh}
sudo dkp-pacman -S --needed 3ds-dev 3ds-zlib 3ds-curl 3ds-mbedtls

export DEVKITPRO=/opt/devkitpro
export DEVKITARM=$DEVKITPRO/devkitARM
export PATH=$DEVKITPRO/tools/bin:$DEVKITARM/bin:$PATH
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

Root is not needed: the packages are plain tarballs @m_span{m-text m-dim}
(@cpp "https://pkg.devkitpro.org/packages/" @ce, listed in the `dkp-libs` and `dkp-linux`
databases) @m_endspan that `bsdtar` extracts into any prefix; point `DEVKITPRO` at the `opt/devkitpro`
inside it. The server answers generic download clients with <b>403</b> --- send pacman's `User-Agent`
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (`pacman/6.1.0 (Linux x86_64) libalpm/14.0.0` works) @m_endspan.

The toolchain file @cpp "$DEVKITPRO/cmake/3DS.cmake" @ce defines `NINTENDO_3DS` and `__3DS__`, links
through `3dsx.specs` and provides `ctr_add_shader_library()`, `ctr_generate_smdh()` and
`ctr_create_3dsx()`.

-   <b>Threads</b> come from devkitARM's `libsysbase` over libctru's `LightLock`/`CondVar`, so
    `find_package(Threads)` succeeds and `NCINE_WITH_THREADS` stays on. `sched_yield()` is supplied by
    @cpp "Sources/nCine/Backends/Ctr/CtrLibcCompat.cpp" @ce; scheduling parameters and affinity are
    absent and unused (the application sees one core).
-   <b>libctru's sub-headers have no `extern "C"`</b>, only the umbrella @cpp <3ds.h> @ce wraps them.
    C++ code that includes @cpp <3ds/svc.h> @ce or @cpp <3ds/synchronization.h> @ce directly must wrap
    the include itself, or the link fails on mangled symbols.

Module music is <b>libxmp</b>, built from source by the configure step; libopenmpt is too heavy for this
CPU.

@subsection consoles-3ds-build Building

@code{.sh}
export DEVKITPRO=/opt/devkitpro
export DEVKITARM=$DEVKITPRO/devkitARM
export PATH=$DEVKITPRO/tools/bin:$DEVKITARM/bin:$PATH
cmake -B ./build/3ds/ -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -D CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=$DEVKITPRO/cmake/3DS.cmake \
    -D NCINE_CONTENT_DIR=$PWD/build/ConsoleContent
cmake --build ./build/3ds/ --parallel $(nproc)
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

The console cannot convert the original data itself, so pass the tree from the AssetPacker's `console`
profile @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref consoles-common-content) @m_endspan. Without it the build falls
back to the repository's `Content`, which boots into the menu but has no levels.

Outputs: @cpp "build/3ds/jazz2.elf" @ce, @cpp "build/3ds/jazz2.3dsx" @ce,
@cpp "build/3ds/jazz2.smdh" @ce @m_span{m-text m-dim} (Homebrew Launcher icon and titles) @m_endspan
and the staged SD card layout:

@code{.shell-session}
build/3ds/sdmc/3ds/Jazz2/Jazz2.3dsx
build/3ds/sdmc/3ds/Jazz2/Jazz2.smdh
build/3ds/sdmc/3ds/Jazz2/Content/…
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

Content is read from @cpp "sdmc:/3ds/Jazz2/Content/" @ce; the cache, configuration, saves and
`Jazz2.log` are written next to it @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref Jazz2::ContentResolver) @m_endspan.

@subsection consoles-3ds-deploy Deploying and running

-   <b>Real hardware</b> --- copy @cpp "build/3ds/sdmc/3ds/Jazz2" @ce to @cpp "/3ds/Jazz2" @ce on the
    SD card and start it from the Homebrew Launcher (needs custom firmware or another way to run
    unsigned homebrew). `3dslink -a <console IP> build/3ds/jazz2.3dsx` sends a fresh build to the
    launcher's network loader; the content must already be on the card.
-   <b>Azahar</b> @m_span{m-text m-dim} (Citra's continuation) @m_endspan ---
    `azahar -w build/3ds/jazz2.3dsx`. Copy the staged @cpp "3ds/Jazz2" @ce tree into its virtual SD
    card @cpp "user/sdmc/" @ce @m_span{m-text m-dim} (flatpak:
    @cpp "~/.var/app/org.azahar_emu.Azahar/data/azahar-emu/sdmc/" @ce) @m_endspan;
    @cpp "sdmc:/" @ce resolves against that card, not the `.3dsx` directory. Networking needs no setup,
    `soc:u` runs on the host's sockets.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par Sound needs the DSP firmware
`ndspInit()` loads @cpp "sdmc:/3ds/dspfirm.cdc" @ce, which homebrew cannot ship; users dump it once
with the `DSP1` homebrew. Without it the game logs the failure and runs silent. In Azahar (HLE audio)
an empty file of a few kilobytes at that path on its virtual card is enough.

@m_class{m-noindent}

<b>Logs:</b> the trace goes to `svcOutputDebugString()`, which Azahar writes to
@cpp "user/log/azahar_log.txt" @ce under `Debug.Emulated` --- <b>hidden by the default filter</b>;
set `log_filter=*:Info Debug.Emulated:Debug` in its `qt-config.ini`. `Jazz2.log` on the virtual card
is flushed as it is written.

Azahar renderers:

-   OpenGL needs <b>OpenGL 4.3</b>, Vulkan a real driver. Without either (e.g. a VM),
    `LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1` gives it Mesa <b>llvmpipe</b> (a 4.5 context, full speed).
-   Its own software renderer @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`graphics_api=0`) @m_endspan is correct but runs
    the menu at about 1 fps --- screenshots only.

@subsection consoles-3ds-limits Limits and known issues

-   <b>Textures are sampled bottom-up</b> (`v = 0` reads the last row in memory), so
    @cpp PicaTexture::BuildPage() @ce stores the source's first row last. Render targets need no extra
    flip: @cpp PicaDevice::ApplyDrawTarget() @ce sends a pass's first raster row to `v = 0`, so targets
    are top-down like on every other backend @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref nCine::RHI) @m_endspan and
    non-power-of-two padding lies below the image.
-   <b>No palette textures.</b> R8/RG8 (palette-indexed) stores are baked to RGBA4 on the CPU per palette
    row, two baked rows kept per texture @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp PicaTexture::EnsureBakedStore() @ce)
    @m_endspan. A sprite drawn with three or more palette rows in one frame rebakes on every draw (logged
    once).
-   <b>Textures are 8..1024 texels, powers of two, 8x8 Morton-tiled, in the linear heap.</b> Larger ones
    are paged as on the GE. The hardware wraps at the padded size, so repeating textures must be powers of
    two (as on the PSP). Stores and vertex buffers must be written back with `GSPGPU_FlushDataCache()`
    before the GPU reads them; vertices are flushed once per submitted command list
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp PicaDevice::FlushPendingWrites() @ce) @m_endspan, not per draw ---
    that GSP request is expensive.
-   <b>Triangle strips join the batch as triangle lists</b>, so the warped textured background batches
    like quads. The PICA200 repeats the 256x256 target it samples in hardware, so each band is one strip,
    with the horizon tint folded in by the combiner @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see
    @ref shader-compiler-fixed-function) @m_endspan.
-   <b>16-bit colour everywhere:</b> RGB565 screen target, RGBA4/RGB565 textures, a 4-bit lightmap. The
    banding is the hardware's.
-   <b>One 400x240 view</b> on the top screen: no stereoscopic 3D, nothing on the bottom screen but the
    console, no rendering-resolution option. The <b>"Sample Rate"</b> option in Options > Sounds exists
    as on the PSP; the DSP resamples to its own 32728 Hz, so the presets go up to that.
-   <b>Text input uses the system `swkbd` applet</b>, which blocks its thread and must not run inside a
    citro3d frame. `MainApplication::ShowScreenKeyboard()` only records the request;
    `UpdateScreenKeyboard()` runs the applet after the frame is presented (the game pauses) and replaces
    the field's text, as on the PSP and Vita. Afterwards the pad reads idle until every button has been
    released once.
-   <b>A mid-frame clear splits the command list.</b> `C3D_RenderTargetClear()` is a transfer-engine fill,
    not a command, so @cpp PicaDevice::Clear() @ce calls `C3D_FrameSplit()` first; a scissored clear is
    drawn as a quad.
-   <b>Online multiplayer works, over ENet only.</b> libctru's sockets @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`soc:u`)
    @m_endspan have `getaddrinfo()` and `poll()`; the vendored `enet.h` only routes `sendmsg()` and
    `recvmsg()` through `sendto()` (buffers gathered into one datagram) and `recvfrom()`. `ENET_IPV6=0`
    (no `in6_addr` in libctru) and `WITH_WEBSOCKET` is off (no @cpp "netinet/ip.h" @ce).
    `CtrPlatform::Initialize()` lends the service a page-aligned 1 MB buffer with `socInit()` before any
    socket opens, and `Shutdown()` takes it back. The WLAN is the system's business; an offline console
    simply fails to connect. In Azahar a server on `127.0.0.1` is the host.
-   <b>A failed socket call is not treated as a lost connection.</b> The one `soc:u` buffer is shared by
    every socket and HTTPS request, so transient errors happen on hardware (not in Azahar). The 3DS arms
    of `enet_socket_send()` and `enet_socket_receive()` treat every error except `EBADF`, `ENOTSOCK` and
    `EFAULT` as nothing sent/received; ENet resends reliable data and the peer timeout still catches a
    dead server. The first 16 errors are logged with their `errno`.
-   <b>HTTPS needs the CA bundle staged next to the content.</b> devkitPro's libcurl (mbedTLS) defaults to
    @cpp "sdmc:/config/ssl/cacert.pem" @ce; the build downloads the bundle at configure time and stages it
    as @cpp "Content/cacert.pem" @ce, where `ApplyPlatformWebRequestOptions()` points libcurl (as on the
    PSP and Vita). Without it the update check and server list fail with an mbedTLS `-0x3E00` error
    naming the default path. The console clock is real time @m_span{m-text m-dim} (unlike pspdev's, see
    @ref consoles-psp-limits) @m_endspan. When porting further: `WebRequest.cpp` picks its libcurl
    backend from an explicit target list, and a missing target silently gets no backend.
-   Asynchronous tracing is off, as on the PSP, so the logs are complete when the process dies.
-   The lighting approximation and @cpp sinApprox() @ce use the PSP's values, untuned on hardware; the
    port was verified in Azahar.

- - -

@section consoles-psp PlayStation Portable

A 222 MHz MIPS Allegrex @m_span{m-text m-dim} (raised to 333 MHz at startup) @m_endspan with 32 MB of
memory @m_span{m-text m-dim} (24 MB usable, 64 MB on the 2000/3000 models) @m_endspan and a Graphics
Engine driven through `sceGu`. The port renders through the `GU` backend @m_span{m-text m-dim}
(@cpp "Sources/nCine/Graphics/RHI/GU" @ce) @m_endspan, presented by the `Psp` window backend at
480x272.

@subsection consoles-psp-toolchain Toolchain

pspdev ships prebuilt for Linux, macOS and Windows, builds from source with `build-all.sh`, and is the
`pspdev/pspdev` container image @m_span{m-text m-dim} (used by CI) @m_endspan:

@code{.sh}
# Prebuilt SDK (see https://github.com/pspdev/pspdev/releases)
tar -xJf pspdev-<platform>.tar.xz -C ~/sdk

export PSPDEV=~/sdk/pspdev
export PATH=$PSPDEV/bin:$PATH
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

The SDK includes <b>zlib</b>, <b>libvorbis</b> and an <b>OpenAL Soft</b> with a `sceAudio` backend;
further ports install with `psp-pacman`. The toolchain file is
@cpp "$PSPDEV/psp/share/pspdev.cmake" @ce (`psp-cmake` passes it automatically). Keep `$PSPDEV/bin` on
`PATH`: packaging runs `psp-fixup-imports`, `mksfoex` and `pack-pbp` from there.

@subsection consoles-psp-build Building

@code{.sh}
export PSPDEV=~/sdk/pspdev
export PATH=$PSPDEV/bin:$PATH
cmake -B ./build/psp/ -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -D CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=$PSPDEV/psp/share/pspdev.cmake \
    -D NCINE_CONTENT_DIR=$PWD/build/ConsoleContent
make -j $(nproc) -C ./build/psp/
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

`Release` compiles with `-O2` as on the other consoles (the FPU is single-precision only). The build
produces @cpp "build/psp/jazz2.elf" @ce and stages the memory-stick layout under
@cpp "build/psp/ms0/" @ce:

@code{.shell-session}
build/psp/ms0/PSP/GAME/Jazz2/EBOOT.PBP
build/psp/ms0/PSP/GAME/Jazz2/Content/…
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

`create_pbp_file()` packs `EBOOT.PBP` from the stripped ELF with `MEMSIZE=1` in its SFO, which requests
the extra memory of the 2000/3000 models.

@subsection consoles-psp-deploy Deploying and running

-   <b>Real hardware</b> --- copy @cpp "build/psp/ms0/PSP/GAME/Jazz2" @ce onto the memory stick at
    exactly that path. Needs custom firmware or another way to start unsigned homebrew.
-   <b>PPSSPP</b> --- copy the same directory into the emulator's <b>configured memory stick</b> and
    start it from the game list.

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par PPSSPP boots from its own memory stick, not from the path passed to it
A path outside the memory stick can silently run a <b>stale</b> copy. Copy the staged tree in every
time --- for the flatpak, @cpp "~/.var/app/org.ppsspp.PPSSPP/config/ppsspp/PSP/GAME/Jazz2/" @ce.

@m_class{m-noindent}

<b>Logs:</b> the trace reaches PPSSPP's log at <em>info</em> level, prefixed
@cpp "I[PRINTF]: stdout:" @ce --- use `--loglevel=4 --log=FILE`; for the flatpak the log path must be
sandbox-writable (`--filesystem=<dir>`). `Jazz2.log` next to the EBOOT is flushed only on a clean exit.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par Networking in PPSSPP
Turn on <b>Enable networking/WLAN</b> for the update check and online play; otherwise
`sceNetApctlConnect()` fails with @cpp "SCE_NET_APCTL_ERROR_WLAN_SWITCH_OFF" @ce. The emulator takes a
little over <b>6 seconds</b> to reach `GOT_IP`, hence the generous wait in
`PspNetwork::ScopedConnection`. PPSSPP lacks `sceNetInetRecvmsg()`, so ENet receives through
`recvfrom()` here.

@m_class{m-noindent}

The game raises the CPU to 333 MHz and installs its HOME-button callback before anything else can fail,
so the console always has a way out.

@subsection consoles-psp-limits Limits and known issues

<b>Crashes, memory and threads</b>

-   <b>FPU exception traps are disabled, and must stay so.</b> `PspDisableFpuTraps()`
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`MainApplication.cpp`) @m_endspan clears the FCSR enable bits
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (bits 7-11, plus the cause and sticky-flag fields) @m_endspan at startup and on
    every engine thread (FCSR is per-thread). With traps armed, a float division by zero kills the
    process instantly: all threads gone, nothing logged, a frozen screen for about ten seconds and
    `C1-2858-3` on a Vita running Adrenaline. Divisors from level data are still guarded
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@ref Jazz2::Actors::Solid::Bridge) @m_endspan.
-   <b>Compute animation frames through @ref Jazz2::GraphicResource::GetFrameForTime()</b>, which returns
    the first frame for a static image; @cpp animTime * FrameCount / AnimDuration @ce divides by zero
    there. `ActorBase::ActorRenderer::UpdateAnimation()` guards zero-duration looping animations. Code
    merely sloppy on a desktop can be fatal here without a log; localize such a crash by enabling parts in
    stages and logging each.
-   <b>A GE store must not be freed while a display list still references it.</b> Lists run only at
    `sceGuFinish()` in `GuDevice::PresentFrame()`, so `GuDevice::SyncBeforeStoreRelease()` kicks the list
    and waits for the GE before `GuTexture` frees a store @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`RefreshGeStore()`,
    `FreeGeStores()`, the destructor) @m_endspan; `EnsureList()` opens a fresh one. Otherwise
    `sceGuSync()` hangs and the firmware kills the process (same freeze and `C1-2858-3`, no log).
-   <b>Main memory is tight.</b> The heap is the whole user partition @m_span{m-text m-dim}
    (`PSP_HEAP_SIZE_KB(-1)`) @m_endspan and a full level leaves a few hundred kilobytes, so even a second
    64 KB streaming vertex buffer may fail. With `-fno-exceptions` a failed `operator new` is a bare
    `abort()`; the `std::set_new_handler` from @ref nCine::Application::InitCommon() logs a fatal line
    first (on every console). Large true-colour tilesets do not fit: the LayerFX test level
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`Sources/Jazz2/Tests/LayerEffects`, a 9.6 MB 32-bit tileset) @m_endspan loses
    tiles from its green screen on, as 512 KB atlas-page stores fail to allocate. PPSSPP's allocator leaves
    more room --- check memory on hardware.
-   <b>Palette-indexed fonts are kept on a palette change</b> (with `RHI_CAP_PALETTED_TEXTURES` they
    resolve colours from the live palette); @ref Jazz2::ContentResolver asks
    @ref Jazz2::UI::Font::IsPaletteIndexed(), saving a texture reload during level load.
-   <b>The GE display list is 32 KB</b> (the busiest frame uses 3.7 KB). `sceGu` does not bounds-check
    it, so `GuDevice::PresentFrame()` warns once if a frame passes three quarters.
-   <b>Threads are on</b>, over pspdev's <b>pthread-embedded</b>. `pthread_setname_np` and CPU affinity
    are compiled out, as on the libogc consoles. `pthread_t` is a pointer (use `reinterpret_cast`), and
    stack information comes from `sceKernelReferThreadStatus()`.
-   <b>Thread stacks are 64 KB, and raising them is not free.</b> `sceKernelCreateThread` allocates stacks
    from what the firmware kept back, which holds one 256 KB stack but not two; the second creation fails
    with `NO_MEMORY` and the thread silently never runs. Even at 64 KB the multiplayer client thread did
    not fit next to the audio decoder and the discovery thread, so the server list stops discovering
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@ref Jazz2::Multiplayer::ServerDiscovery::Stop()) @m_endspan before it
    connects, and a client whose thread cannot start reports the failure.
-   <b>Spinlocks sleep here.</b> The kernel has strict priorities and no time-slicing, so a busy-wait
    deadlocks against a descheduled holder; @ref Death::Threading::Spinlock sleeps
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`sceKernelDelayThread(50)`) @m_endspan while contended. The multiplayer
    client thread raises itself above the main thread's priority 32 @m_span{m-text m-dim}
    (`sceKernelChangeThreadPriority(0, 24)`) @m_endspan, otherwise pings are 200-300 ms. The console's
    WLAN power saving (a system setting) adds latency too.

<b>Rendering</b>

-   Textures are limited to <b>512 pixels per axis</b>; larger atlases are split into pages with
    per-primitive page selection. Palettized textures use the hardware CLUT, only one of which is resident
    at a time, so the palette is part of the batch key.
-   The GE has no post-texture additive term and no combiner output scale: an `offset_color` pass expands
    into a modulated draw plus an additive silhouette, and the `MODULATE_X2`/`MODULATE_X4` presets are
    rejected for this target.
-   <b>Line strips are expanded into coverage quads</b>, not drawn as `GU_LINE_STRIP`: a triangle pair per
    segment, half a pixel to each side, widened by the slope's Manhattan factor, as on the PowerVR
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref consoles-dreamcast-limits) @m_endspan. They batch with the frame's
    other triangles, and the weapon wheel skips its four half-pixel shadow segments here
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref Jazz2::UI::HUD::DrawWeaponWheel()) @m_endspan.
-   <b>The race minimap's track is rasterized on the CPU</b> (the fixed-function tier has no mesh-sprite
    program): @ref Jazz2::UI::Multiplayer::MpHUD::RefreshMinimapTrackTexture() renders it once into an
    alpha mask, drawn as two sprites (track and shadow).
-   <b>Dynamic lighting is splatted on the VFPU.</b> The PSP arm of
    `CombineRenderer::PrepareSoftwareLighting()` is inline VFPU assembly, four texels per instruction,
    and must run on the main thread, the only one with the VFPU @m_span{m-text m-dim}
    (`PSP_MAIN_THREAD_ATTR`) @m_endspan. Other consoles use the scalar path in
    @cpp "Rendering/SoftwareLightSplat.h" @ce. Ring lights @m_span{m-text m-dim}
    (@ref Jazz2::LightEmitter::RingRadius) @m_endspan use `SplatRingVfpu()` here,
    `SoftwareLighting::SplatRing()` elsewhere.

<b>Audio and math</b>

-   <b>Module music plays through libxmp.</b> `NCINE_WITH_OPENMPT` is forced off --- libopenmpt's first
    module load takes about 29 seconds on this CPU --- and `NCINE_WITH_XMP` is on by default. libxmp reads
    the original `.j2b` modules directly, but not <b>MO3</b>, so the four `.mo3` tracks are silent. Its
    double-precision hot paths are replaced @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref consoles-common-skips)
    @m_endspan.
-   <b>Audio mixes at 44100 Hz by default; the "Sample Rate" option in Options > Sounds can halve it</b>
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (22050 or 44100 Hz, stored in @ref Jazz2::PreferencesCache::AudioSampleRate,
    applied through @ref nCine::IAudioDevice::setMixingFrequency()) @m_endspan, halving the mixer's and
    the module decoder's cost. @ref nCine::PspAudioDevice mixes at that rate and linearly upsamples below
    44100 Hz; the decoder renders at @ref nCine::IAudioDevice::nativeFrequency(). Effects switch at once,
    music when the next stream opens.
-   <b>This libm is very slow --- `sinf()` costs about 13.5 us.</b> Appearance-only math (flicker, pulse,
    orbit) goes through @ref nCine::sinApprox() / @ref nCine::cosApprox() (331 ns, within 0.0014), which
    substitute only on the consoles because on x86-64 the library is faster. Avoid `std::floor()` too
    (a real `jal floorf`); the remaining calls go to an exact replacement @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see
    @ref consoles-common-skips) @m_endspan.
-   <b>The underwater and pause low-pass filters are absent</b> --- the mixer has no filter stage, like the
    other software-mixing consoles.
-   <b>The Media Engine is not used.</b> pspdev's `libme-core` needs custom firmware, reports the core
    unavailable under Adrenaline (no ME firmware image) and hangs PPSSPP in `kinit()`; code touching the ME
    must first test for the emulator with @cpp sceIoDevctl("kemulator:", 0x03, ...) @ce, which succeeds
    only there.

<b>System services</b>

-   <b>The C library's clock is replaced.</b> pspdev's libcglue implements `_gettimeofday()` (behind
    `time()`, `gettimeofday()` and `std::chrono::system_clock`) as seconds since midnight, i.e. 1 January
    1970, so mbedTLS rejected every certificate as not yet valid --- and pspdev's libcurl
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (7.64.1 on mbedTLS 2.28) @m_endspan reports it without detail.
    @cpp "Sources/nCine/Backends/Psp/PspLibcCompat.cpp" @ce defines `_gettimeofday()` from the RTC tick,
    overriding libcglue's.
-   <b>No timezone in the C library</b>, so `localtime()` is `gmtime()`. `GetLocalTimeZoneOffset()`
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`DateTime.cpp`) @m_endspan gets the offset, daylight saving included, by
    passing one tick through `sceRtcConvertUtcToLocalTime()`, and `GetLocalTm()` applies it.
-   <b>HTTPS needs its own CA bundle.</b> The configure step downloads curl.se's `cacert.pem` and stages it
    under `Content/`, as on the @ref consoles-vita-limits "Vita";
    @cpp "Sources/Jazz2/PlatformWebRequest.h" @ce points `CURLOPT_CAINFO` at it and <b>clears
    `CURLOPT_CAPATH`</b>, because mbedTLS reads the default directory (`/etc/ssl/certs`) first and fails on
    it. To debug a failed verification, route `CURLOPT_DEBUGFUNCTION` into the trace.
-   <b>Text is entered through the firmware's on-screen keyboard.</b>
    @ref nCine::MainApplication::ShowScreenKeyboard() opens the modal `sceUtility` OSK and
    @ref nCine::MainApplication::UpdateScreenKeyboard() collects the string.
    @ref nCine::RHI::GU::GuDevice::PresentFrame() refreshes the dialog between list completion and the
    flip, and @ref nCine::Backends::PspInputManager reports the pad idle while it is open (else the
    confirming Cross also fires the menu item behind it). The status call returns an error, not "none",
    when no keyboard was started, so checks match dialog states explicitly.
-   <b>The PSPSDK stub libraries must not be linked twice.</b> psp-gcc's `*lib` spec already appends
    `-lpsputility -lpsprtc -lpspnet_inet -lpspnet_resolver -lpspsdk -lpspmodinfo -lpspuser`; adding any of
    them to `target_link_libraries` splits their stubs, `psp-fixup-imports` reports
    @cpp "could not fixup imports, stubs out of order" @ce, and syscalls such as `sceNetInetSocket()`
    end up calling something else.

<b>Networking</b>

-   <b>Online multiplayer works, over ENet only.</b> `WITH_WEBSOCKET` is forced off (pspdev has neither
    @cpp "netinet/ip.h" @ce nor @cpp "poll.h" @ce) and `ENET_IPV6=0` (`sceNetInet` has no IPv6). The
    vendored @cpp "Sources/Dependencies/enet/enet.h" @ce supplies the `poll()`, `getaddrinfo()` and
    `getnameinfo()` libcglue lacks, over `select()` and `gethostbyname()`/`gethostbyaddr()`. Non-blocking
    sockets use `setsockopt(SO_NONBLOCK)`, since `fcntl()` does not reach the firmware's sockets, and
    receiving uses `recvfrom()` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref consoles-psp-deploy) @m_endspan.
-   <b>Ad hoc (local wireless) multiplayer is available next to Wi-Fi.</b> Peers are MAC address + port
    over PDP sockets (`sceNetAdhocPdp*`), and a session is a <em>group</em> found by scanning. It is a
    second arm of ENet's socket functions (`enet_psp_set_adhoc()`), where an `ENetAddress` host indexes a
    table of seen MAC addresses, written `AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF`.
    @ref nCine::Backends::PspNetwork::AdhocBegin() drops the access point and brings up
    `sceNetAdhoc`/`sceNetAdhocctl`; the menus switch modes through
    @ref Jazz2::Multiplayer::NetworkManagerBase::SetAdhocMode().
    -   <b>Hosting:</b> the <em>Connection</em> row of <em>Create Server</em> picks Wi-Fi or ad hoc; the
        group is named after the server (8 alphanumeric characters, see
        @ref nCine::Backends::PspNetwork::MakeAdhocGroupName()).
    -   <b>Joining:</b> Left/Right in <em>Connect To Server</em> toggles the mode;
        @ref Jazz2::Multiplayer::ServerDiscovery then lists scanned groups, and an endpoint is `GROUP/MAC`.
    -   Ad hoc groups are visible only to other PSPs (and Vitas running Adrenaline); an ad hoc server is
        not published anywhere.
-   <b>The access point is joined on demand.</b> `PspNetwork::Initialize()` @m_span{m-text m-dim}
    (@cpp "Sources/nCine/Backends/Psp/PspNetwork.cpp" @ce) @m_endspan loads the network PRXes at startup;
    `PspNetwork::ScopedConnection`, held by each network consumer on its own thread, joins and waits for
    DHCP. The association is reference counted; the last release starts a <b>30 second</b> grace period,
    ended by `PspNetwork::Update()` once a frame. The infrastructure modules stay loaded (unloading them
    under a live libcurl session is unsafe).
-   <b>The access point comes from the saved connections.</b> The slots under <em>Network Settings</em>
    are probed and the first existing one is used (deleting a connection leaves its slot empty). The
    Vita's PSP emulator @m_span{m-text m-dim} (Adrenaline, ARK-4) @m_endspan saves none, so the
    <em>connect automatically</em> profile, <b>configuration 0</b>, is used instead; a real console with
    nothing saved refuses it and reports that no connection is configured.
-   <b>Local discovery uses the IPv4 broadcast address</b> `255.255.255.255`
    (`TryCreateLocalSocket()`). Desktop builds use `ff02::1` multicast plus an `AF_INET` broadcast socket
    (`TryCreateLocalBroadcastSocket()`; the multicast one is `IPV6_V6ONLY` since they share a port), so
    console and desktop find each other. @ref Jazz2::UI::Menu::ServerSelectSection merges answers with the
    same identifier into one entry with `|`-joined endpoints.

- - -

@section consoles-ps2 PlayStation 2

A 294 MHz MIPS R5900 "Emotion Engine" with 32 MB of main memory and a Graphics Synthesizer whose
<b>4 MB</b> of local memory is the frame buffer, texture cache and palette store at once. The port
renders through the `GS` backend @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp "Sources/nCine/Graphics/RHI/GS" @ce)
@m_endspan, presented by the `Ps2` window backend in NTSC 640x448.

@subsection consoles-ps2-toolchain Toolchain

Use the `ps2dev/ps2dev` container image, which the recipe below assumes. It ships no CMake, so derive one:

@code{.docker}
FROM docker.io/ps2dev/ps2dev:latest
RUN apk add --no-cache cmake make git python3 bash xorriso
@endcode

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par $PS2DEV/ee/bin is not on PATH
Not even in a login shell, so CMake fails with "mips64r5900el-ps2-elf-g++ is not a full path and was not
found". Export `PATH=$PS2DEV/ee/bin:$PS2DEV/bin:$PS2SDK/bin:$PATH` before configuring. `xorriso` (above)
authors the disc image.

@subsection consoles-ps2-content Preparing the content tree

The console boots from a read-only disc with no first-run conversion and nothing staged into `Cache/`, so
the tree must be complete before the image is authored. Prepare it as in @ref consoles-common-content,
with the `ps2` profile:

@code{.sh}
# 1. The tool (any desktop build directory already has it)
cmake -B ./build/ -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build ./build/ --target AssetPacker --parallel $(nproc)

# 2. Convert - the original game data and the game's own content are named separately
./build/Utilities/AssetPacker/AssetPacker ./build/ConsoleContentPs2 \
    --source=<path to the original Jazz Jackrabbit 2> --content=./Content --target=ps2

# 3. Prebaked.pak is what makes it a prepared tree
ls ./build/ConsoleContentPs2
@endcode

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par Why `--target=ps2` and not `--target=console`
Only the cinematics differ: `console` keeps the original 640x480 zlib `.j2v`, too slow to inflate here;
`ps2` re-encodes them into the game's LZ4 container @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref Jazz2::VideoFormat)
@m_endspan at <b>320x240</b> (see @ref asset-packer-cinematics-format; any profile can do this with
`--video-downscale=`). A `console` tree still plays, just slower, and a `ps2` tree works on the other
consoles too.

@m_class{m-noindent}

Pass the result as `NCINE_CONTENT_DIR` on the first configuration of the build directory. The packaging
step assembles the disc under @cpp "build/ps2/cd/" @ce and authors the image:

@code{.shell-session}
build/ps2/cd/SYSTEM.CNF     # BOOT2 = cdrom0:\JAZZ2.ELF;1, VER, VMODE = NTSC
build/ps2/cd/CDFS.IRX       # copied from the SDK; loaded at startup so newlib can see the disc
build/ps2/cd/JAZZ2.ELF      # the stripped executable
build/ps2/cd/Content/…      # NCINE_CONTENT_DIR, with "Source.pak" renamed if it has one
@endcode

@code{.sh}
xorrisofs -quiet -iso-level 2 -l -J -V JAZZ2 -o ./build/ps2/jazz2.iso ./build/ps2/cd
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

`mkisofs` and `genisoimage` are accepted instead, with the same options. ISO 9660 level 2 with `-l`
limits file names on the disc to <b>31 characters</b> --- mind this for custom levels or tilesets.

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par "No Source.pak" during configure is expected here
The packaging step renames a @cpp "Source.pak" @ce to @cpp "Prebaked.pak" @ce (so a `desktop`-profile
cache can go on a disc too). A `console`/`ps2` tree already has @cpp "Prebaked.pak" @ce, so configure
prints `No "Source.pak" in "<path>", the disc image will not be marked as prebaked` --- harmless. Check
for @cpp "Prebaked.pak" @ce in the tree instead; if neither package is there, the conversion never ran
or the wrong directory was passed.

@m_class{m-block m-danger}

@par The staging directory is added to, never cleared
As on the Dreamcast, @cpp "cmake -E copy_directory" @ce keeps files no longer in the source, so anything
ever staged into @cpp "build/ps2/cd/" @ce ends up in every later image. After regenerating the content
tree, `rm -rf ./build/ps2/cd/` and build again.

@m_class{m-noindent}

@cpp "Music" @ce belongs on the disc: the soundtrack plays through <b>libxmp</b>
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref consoles-ps2-limits) @m_endspan.

@subsection consoles-ps2-storage Running from a USB stick or an MX4SIO SD adapter

A <b>USB stick</b> in a front port or an <b>MX4SIO</b> adapter (an SD card on a controller port) work as
alternatives to the disc, with the same build: the game looks for a content tree on them at startup and
then reads and writes everything there @m_span{m-text m-dim} (a memory card is too small for the level
cache) @m_endspan. Two layouts are looked for, in this order:

-   <b>A `Content` directory next to the executable</b>, on any device and in any directory, taken from
    @cpp argv[0] @ce @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @cpp nCine::Backends::Ps2Storage::SetBootPath() @ce)
    @m_endspan.
-   <b>`Games/Jazz2/` on a mounted unit</b>, so the tree does not collide with a loader's own `APPS`,
    `DVD` and `CFG` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (the same layout as the Switch's
    @cpp "sdmc:/Games/Jazz2/" @ce) @m_endspan.

@code{.shell-session}
mass0:/Games/Jazz2/JAZZ2.ELF      # wherever the loader is pointed at; this is the tidy place for it
mass0:/Games/Jazz2/Content/…      # what the probe looks for
mass0:/Games/Jazz2/Cache/…        # created by the game, unlike on a disc
mass0:/Games/Jazz2/Jazz2.config   # likewise
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

Units `mass0:` to `mass3:` are probed in order. The IOP modules needed --- `bdm`, `bdmfs_fatfs`
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (FAT12/16/32 and exFAT) @m_endspan, `mx4sio_bd`, `usbd` + `usbmass_bd`, and
`audsrv` --- are <b>linked into the executable</b>, since a device's only driver cannot be read from it
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @cpp nCine::Backends::Ps2Modules @ce) @m_endspan.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par What the loader already mounted is left alone
The units are probed with one `fioDopen()` each before any module is loaded: the loader that started
the executable (wLaunchELF, Open PS2 Loader) already has the driver for its device resident, and a second
`mx4sio_bd` would clock the same SIO2 registers against the first.
@par
Loading modules from EE memory patches a running ROM service and is untested on hardware, so <b>a disc
boot never does it</b>: `audsrv` is loaded from the disc next to `CDFS.IRX` and the block-device stack
is not loaded at all @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @cpp nCine::Backends::Ps2Modules::Load() @ce)
@m_endspan.

@m_class{m-noindent}

@cpp nCine::Backends::Ps2Storage @ce only brings the modules up and reports which units mounted; the
directory name and the `Content`/`Cache`/`Source` layout live in
@cpp Jazz2::ContentResolver::InitializePaths() @ce. A build running from removable storage writes
`Jazz2.log` next to its content --- the only practical way to read a trace without a serial cable. From a
disc no log is written (the memory card is no place for one).

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par A disc in the drive always wins
The probe runs only when the drive reports <b>no disc at all</b>, so a disc boot never picks up a stale
tree from a card or touches a driver that shares the SIO2 registers with `SIO2MAN`, `MCMAN` and the pads.
To run from removable storage, start the ELF from it with no disc in the drive; a disc image launched
through Open PS2 Loader reads its content from the image.
@par
`sceCdInit()` returns before the drive has identified the disc, and `SCECdNODISC` is the <b>zero</b>
media type, so an early read says "no disc" on a console booting from one --- the startup then skips
`CDFS.IRX` and shows a black screen with nothing logged. The drive is given @cpp DiscTypeSettleMs @ce
before an empty tray is believed.

@subsection consoles-ps2-build Building

@code{.sh}
cmake -S . -B build/ps2 -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=$PS2SDK/ps2dev.cmake \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DNCINE_STRIP_BINARIES=ON \
    -DNCINE_CONTENT_DIR=$PWD/build/ConsoleContent
cmake --build build/ps2 -j$(nproc)
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

The toolchain file defines `PLATFORM_PS2`, which pins `NCINE_PREFERRED_RHI` to `GS`.
`NCINE_CONTENT_DIR` is cached, so set it on the first configuration @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see
@ref consoles-ps2-content) @m_endspan. The build produces a stripped `jazz2.elf` and a bootable
`jazz2.iso` with the layout above; `Source.pak` is renamed to `Prebaked.pak` so the game sees a prepared
tree, and nothing goes into `Cache/`.

Without an ISO author on `PATH` at configure time the build prints
`xorrisofs/mkisofs not found, bootable ISO image will not be created` and produces only the ELF. That
boots in PCSX2 with `-elf`, but with no disc there is no content --- the game reads everything from
@cpp "cdfs:/" @ce.

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par The image must carry a Joliet hierarchy (`-J`)
Plain ISO 9660 mangles names (`xmas 1 feaw.j2t` becomes `XMAS_1_FEAW.J2T`), which affects 42 files of the
content tree --- most christmas tilesets and some music. ps2sdk's `cdfs.irx` reads the real names from
Joliet only and ignores Rock Ridge, `mkisofs`'s default @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see `cdfs_iop.c`)
@m_endspan. Without `-J` those levels come up without their tileset and nothing is logged.

@subsection consoles-ps2-swap Changing the content of a finished image

As on the @ref consoles-dreamcast-swap "Dreamcast", the content can be replaced without rebuilding the
image:

@code{.sh}
./build/Utilities/AssetPacker/AssetPacker swap-content ./build/ps2/jazz2.iso \
    --content=./build/ConsoleContent
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

`swap-content` keeps `SYSTEM.CNF`, the executable it names and the `.IRX` modules as they are and
rewrites the file system around the new `Content`; the image is resized to fit. It always writes a Joliet
hierarchy. See @ref asset-packer-swap-content.

@subsection consoles-ps2-deploy Deploying and running

Burn `jazz2.iso` to a disc, or load it in PCSX2. A headless run needs the setup wizard marked complete
and a BIOS filename set in its INI, `EnableEEConsole` on to see any output, and a renderer the host GPU
can create:

@code{.sh}
pcsx2-qt -batch -nogui -earlyconsolelog -logfile run.log jazz2.iso
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

<b>Logs:</b> the trace goes to the Emotion Engine's <b>SIO transmit register</b>
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (`0x1000F180`) @m_endspan, including the early CDVD bring-up messages from
@cpp MainApplication::Run() @ce. PCSX2 captures it as EE console output; on hardware a serial cable
picks it up.

Without a cable the same lines appear on the <b>television</b> (PS2SDK's libdebug console, opened
before the IOP is touched) until @cpp Ps2GfxDevice @ce logs "Handing the display over to the renderer".
Every IOP step that can stall a boot (disc wait, `CDFS.IRX`, memory card, `LIBSD` and `audsrv`, the
MX4SIO probe) runs before that, so the last line shown names the stuck step; a hang after it points at
renderer setup or content loading. Report the last lines shown when a boot stops.

Before the console exists, solid screen colours mark the earlier stages @m_span{m-text m-dim}
(`Ps2BootMarker()` in @cpp "MainApplication.cpp" @ce, via `PMODE = 0` and `BGCOLOR`) @m_endspan. A
screen stuck on one of them names the stage that did not come back:

Screen       | Set when
------------ | --------
<b>Red</b>   | Static initialization begins
<b>Green</b> | @cpp main() @ce reaches @cpp MainApplication::Run() @ce
<b>Blue</b>  | The trace system is up and the boot console is about to open
Black        | Never --- none of the executable ran: the loader, the file or the video signal

@subsection consoles-ps2-limits Limits and known issues

-   <b>Local memory is the binding constraint.</b> The 640x448 `PSMCT16` display buffer, render-target
    reserve and CLUT slab leave about <b>2816 KB</b> of texture cache. A frame's working set fits, a whole
    level's sheets do not, so textures stream on transitions and a busy level still reports allocation
    failures. The allocator hands out whole 8 KB <b>pages</b>, because the swizzle within a page depends on
    the pixel storage mode.
-   <b>Sound goes through `audsrv`</b>, the IRX that owns the SPU2 on the IOP; the EE only hands it
    finished PCM @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref nCine::Ps2AudioDevice) @m_endspan.
    -   Its ring buffer is small @m_span{m-text m-dim} (about 50 ms at the default 22050 Hz, reported in the
        trace) @m_endspan and there is no mixer thread, so a long frame is heard as a <em>repeat</em> of
        the last buffer. Anything that blocks the main thread longer (a level load, the episode list) must
        call @ref nCine::IAudioDevice::beginBlockingOperation() first, which stops the stream. Gameplay
        below about 19 fps still repeats.
    -   Music is decoded on the main thread in 1 KB slices during the vertical-blank wait
        @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@ref nCine::IAudioDevice::runIdleWork()) @m_endspan; a chunk unfinished
        after twelve frames is decoded inside the frame. libxmp's double-precision hot paths (soft-float on
        the EE) are replaced @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref consoles-common-skips) @m_endspan.
    -   `NCINE_WITH_OPENMPT` is forced off @m_span{m-text m-dim} (libopenmpt's
        `to_chars(char*, char*, const double&)` is ambiguous against PS2SDK's newlib) @m_endspan, so the
        soundtrack is <b>libxmp</b>'s (on by default), which cannot read the 4 `.mo3` tracks of the 56.
-   <b>The picture is composed for 4:3, not for 640x448.</b> An NTSC pixel here is a seventh taller than
    wide; @cpp Ps2GfxDevice::displayAspect() @ce reports that and the view is fitted to it. The view is
    <b>597x448</b> at the default rendering resolution, so nothing is resampled vertically
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @cpp Jazz2::Rendering::UpscaleRenderPass::DefaultViewWidth @ce, which
    raises the bound from the 16:9 720x405 of other platforms) @m_endspan.
-   <b>Threads are off</b>, so multiplayer @m_span{m-text m-dim} (including local splitscreen)
    @m_endspan is compiled out and asset loading is synchronous.
-   <b>Saves need writable storage.</b> Preferences and progress go to a memory card
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`mc0:/Jazz2/`, the first usable slot, with the `icon.sys` the console's browser
    needs or it shows "Corrupted Data") @m_endspan --- or, when booted from removable storage, next to the
    content @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref consoles-ps2-storage) @m_endspan, the only configuration with
    room for the level cache.
-   The GS has no post-texture additive colour term and no combiner output scale: an `offset_color` pass
    expands into a modulated draw plus an additive silhouette @m_span{m-text m-dim} (via a white-RGB CLUT,
    see @cpp GsDevice::AcquireCoverageClutForOffset() @ce) @m_endspan, and the
    `MODULATE_X2`/`MODULATE_X4` presets are rejected for this target.

- - -

@section consoles-ps3 PlayStation 3

A 3.2 GHz Cell PPE with <b>256 MB of XDR</b> main memory and an RSX (NVIDIA NV47) with <b>256 MB of
GDDR3</b>. The port renders through the `RSX` backend
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp "Sources/nCine/Graphics/RHI/RSX" @ce) @m_endspan, presented by the `Ps3`
window backend at whatever mode the display accepts.

The RSX is fully programmable, so unlike the fixed-function `PVR`/`GX`/`GU`/`GS` tier it runs the
whole bloom / lighting / combine / rescale chain. There is no shader compiler on the console, so the Cg
is compiled to NV40 microcode <b>offline</b> and embedded in the executable.

@subsection consoles-ps3-toolchain Toolchain

-   <b>ps3toolchain</b> provides the `powerpc64-ps3-elf` compilers. Use the prebuilt
    `ps3dev-linux-X64.tar.gz` from its nightly releases --- building from source takes hours.
-   <b>PSL1GHT</b> is the SDK (librsx, libgcm_sys, libio, libaudio, libsysutil) plus the host tools
    used for packaging @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`fself`, `make_self_npdrm`, `sfo`, `pkg`, `sprxlinker`,
    `cgcomp`) @m_endspan. Built from source by step 8 of ps3toolchain.
-   <b>NVIDIA's Cg Toolkit</b> is needed only to <em>regenerate</em> the shader table
    (`cgcomp` wraps `libCg.so`), not to build the game. See @ref consoles-ps3-shaders.

@code{.sh}
export PS3DEV=<prefix>          # e.g. ~/sdk/ps3dev
export PSL1GHT=$PS3DEV/psl1ght
export PATH=$PS3DEV/bin:$PS3DEV/ppu/bin:$PS3DEV/spu/bin:$PATH

# Prebuilt compilers
curl -L -o ps3dev.tar.gz https://github.com/ps3dev/ps3toolchain/releases/download/<nightly>/ps3dev-linux-X64.tar.gz
tar xzf ps3dev.tar.gz -C $(dirname $PS3DEV)

# PSL1GHT and the host tools (step 8), then the portlibs (step 9)
git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/ps3dev/ps3toolchain
cd ps3toolchain && ./toolchain.sh 8 9
@endcode

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par ppu-gcc reads $PSL1GHT itself
`PSL1GHT` must be exported in the shell environment of every compile, not just set as a CMake variable,
otherwise the compiler fails with "environment variable 'PSL1GHT' not defined".

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par Don't skip step 9
The portlibs include <b>zlib</b>, which is required --- without it
@ref Death::IO::Compression::DeflateStream "DeflateStream" has no implementation and the game does not
link. They also bring <b>libogg</b> and <b>libvorbis</b>. Step 9 needs `libtool` on the host, which
ps3toolchain does not check for.

@subsection consoles-ps3-build Building

@code{.sh}
cmake -S . -B build/ps3 -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=$PWD/cmake/toolchains/ps3dev.cmake \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -DNCINE_CONTENT_DIR=<path to the console content tree>
cmake --build build/ps3 -j$(nproc)
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

PSL1GHT ships no CMake support, so the toolchain file @cpp "cmake/toolchains/ps3dev.cmake" @ce is the
project's own, not the SDK's. It defines `PLATFORM_PS3`, which pins `NCINE_PREFERRED_RHI` to `RSX`.

The build produces `jazz2.elf` and stages a package tree from a stripped copy:

-   `sprxlinker` rewrites the PRX import stubs, `make_self_npdrm` wraps the result as `EBOOT.BIN`
-   `fself` produces an unsigned `jazz2.self` that RPCS3 and CFW consoles run straight from disk
-   `sfo` writes `PARAM.SFO` with the title and application id @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`JAZZ20000`) @m_endspan
-   the content tree is copied to `USRDIR/Content`, with `Source.pak` renamed to `Prebaked.pak`
-   `pkg` wraps the whole tree into `jazz2.pkg`

The application id is derived from the application name, zero-padded to nine characters and
uppercased, like the PS Vita title id @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref consoles-vita) @m_endspan; the
content id, `PARAM.SFO` and the runtime writable path all follow from it. Override it with
`-DPS3_APPID=<id>` --- anything other than four letters followed by five digits fails configuration.

@subsection consoles-ps3-shaders Shaders are compiled offline

The microcode is generated ahead of time and committed, like the other generated shader headers:

@code{.sh}
PS3DEV=<prefix> LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<Cg toolkit>/usr/lib64 \
    ./build/Utilities/ShaderCompiler/ShaderCompiler --generate-all
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

This is the `--emit-rsx` mode of @ref shader-compiler "ShaderCompiler", run by `--generate-all` when
`cgcomp` is found (through `$PS3DEV/bin`, `PATH`, or `--cgcomp`). It emits Cg from each shader's plain
view, not the Vita's `LOW_POWER_GPU` one @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref shader-compiler-cg-rsx)
@m_endspan, caps `BATCH_SIZE` to fit the RSX's constant registers, and compiles with the `vp40`/`fp40`
profiles. `cgcomp` needs the Cg Toolkit on the library path, hence `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`.

-   Without `cgcomp`, `--generate-all` reports @cpp "skipped: RsxGeneratedShaders.h" @ce and leaves
    the committed header untouched. Commit the result --- the console build never runs `cgcomp`.
-   A stage the profiles reject is left out of the table; the backend then fails that program at load
    time with a message naming the variant. See @ref consoles-ps3-limits for which ones.

@subsection consoles-ps3-deploy Deploying and running

Install `jazz2.pkg` on a console with custom firmware, or run the staged tree directly:

@code{.sh}
rpcs3 --no-gui build/ps3/pkg/USRDIR/EBOOT.BIN
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

RPCS3 needs the PS3 firmware installed once @m_span{m-text m-dim} (<b>File &rarr; Install Firmware</b>,
or `--installfw` with Sony's `PS3UPDAT.PUP`) @m_endspan, otherwise it stops at "Missing Firmware".
Booting `EBOOT.BIN` in place makes `/app_home` resolve to `USRDIR`, so no install step is needed.

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par Headless runs are not representative
`--headless` removes the flip timing the emulator's RSX thread paces itself against, so its watchdog
eventually reports "Dead FIFO commands queue state has been detected" on a healthy title. Use it to
capture a log, but ignore that message. A host with no GPU @m_span{m-text m-dim} (llvmpipe) @m_endspan
or under memory pressure causes the same.

@subsection consoles-ps3-limits Limits and known issues

-   <b>No threads.</b> PSL1GHT's `<pthread.h>` declares symbols nothing implements, so `pthread_create`
    does not link; supporting lv2 PPU threads @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`sysThreadCreate`, `sys/mutex.h`,
    `sys/cond.h`) @m_endspan would need a new backend in @cpp "Thread.cpp" @ce. Multiplayer
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (including local splitscreen) @m_endspan is compiled out and asset loading is
    synchronous.
-   <b>Three rescale filters have no microcode</b> (they exceed `fp40` limits): `ResizeCleanEdge`,
    `ResizeSabr` and `ResizeMonochrome`. `RHI_CAP_HEAVY_RESCALE_SHADERS` is left undefined, so they are
    dropped from <em>Options > Graphics > Rescale Mode</em>; a value saved by another backend falls
    back to pixel-perfect.
-   <b>Sprite batching is off</b> (`RHI_CAP_BATCHING` undefined), so every sprite is drawn individually
    and batched programs are not linked: RPCS3 aborts the batched vertex programs
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`Src check failed. Aborting`) @m_endspan and renders nothing (real hardware
    untested). Patching the emitted microcode breaks constant fetches --- don't.
-   <b>When re-enabled, the batch size is 32</b>, and three places must agree: ShaderCompiler's
    `RsxMaxBatchSize`, @cpp RsxDevice::MaxBatchSize @ce and
    @cpp IRhiCapabilities::IntValues::MaxBatchSize @ce. Left to derive it, the engine picks 65 and
    overruns; the backend reports this at load time.
-   <b>Generated stage attributes are struct-qualified</b> (`_input.aQuadCorner`, not `aQuadCorner`),
    while `rsxVertexProgramGetAttrib()` matches whole names only, so
    @cpp RsxDevice::FindVertexAttribute() @ce matches the name as a suffix. Looking up the bare name
    finds nothing and renders an all-black frame with no error.
-   Otherwise nothing is known to be missing: under RPCS3 the game runs at 60 FPS with everything
    rendering correctly and no FIFO desync.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par Toolchain libstdc++ quirks
`powerpc64-ps3-elf` was built with `_GLIBCXX_USE_C99_MATH_TR1` undefined, so `<cmath>` puts no C99
maths functions in `std::`. Defining the macro breaks `<cmath>` (missing `long double` variants);
instead @cpp "cmake/toolchains/ps3-libstdc++-c99.h" @ce imports the names and is
<b>force-included into every translation unit</b> by the toolchain file, third-party sources included.
@par
The same libstdc++ has no gthreads, so no `std::mutex`. libopenmpt's single-threaded mode can't be
selected with a `-D` (`mpt/base/detect_quirks.hpp` hardcodes it), so @cpp "cmake/Findlibopenmpt.cmake" @ce
patches the fetched copy; hence the PS3 sets `NCINE_COMPILE_OPENMPT` (ps3dev has no libopenmpt and
there is no dynamic loader).

- - -

@section consoles-vita PlayStation Vita

A shader platform with a PowerVR SGX543MP4+ driven by <b>sceGxm</b>; window and input come from
<b>SDL2</b>. It is the only console with a choice of two hardware backends.

`NCINE_PREFERRED_RHI` | What it drives
-------------------- | --------------
`GXM` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (default) @m_endspan | <b>sceGxm directly.</b> Full pipeline (bloom / lighting / combine), without the OpenGL translation layer
`OpenGL` | <b>vitaGL</b>, OpenGL\|ES 2.0 on top of sceGxm. Forces the OpenGL\|ES path and the strict ES 2.0 profile
`Software` | The CPU rasterizer, for comparison and bring-up

@subsection consoles-vita-toolchain Toolchain

Install <b>VitaSDK</b> with `vdpm` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see [vitasdk.org](https://vitasdk.org/)) @m_endspan,
export `VITASDK` and use @cpp "$VITASDK/share/vita.toolchain.cmake" @ce.

@subsection consoles-vita-build Building

@code{.sh}
export VITASDK=/usr/local/vitasdk
cmake -B ./build/vita/ -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -D CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=$VITASDK/share/vita.toolchain.cmake
make -j $(nproc) -C ./build/vita/
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

This builds the `GXM` backend; add @cpp "-D NCINE_PREFERRED_RHI=OpenGL" @ce for vitaGL. Either way the
console needs @cpp "libshacccg.suprx" @ce, see @ref consoles-vita-shaders. The result is
@cpp "build/vita/jazz2.vpk" @ce.

@subsection consoles-vita-deploy Deploying and running

Install the VPK with <b>VitaShell</b> @m_span{m-text m-dim} (or over FTP) @m_endspan. It carries the
@cpp "Content" @ce directory, unpacked into the read-only @cpp "ux0:/app/jazz20000/" @ce
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (mounted as @cpp "app0:" @ce) @m_endspan, so nothing else is needed to start.

The Vita <b>converts the original game data itself</b> on first run: copy an original installation to
@cpp "ux0:/data/jazz2/Source/" @ce and it builds @cpp "ux0:/data/jazz2/Cache/" @ce --- or prepare the
tree with AssetPacker to skip the wait. Reinstalling the VPK leaves the cache, source data and settings
on @cpp "ux0:" @ce alone.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par Vita3K notes
Vita3K does not implement @cpp "sceNetRecvmsg()" @ce, so ENet receives through @cpp "recvfrom()" @ce
and online play works there; the stubbed @cpp "sceNetCtlInit()" @ce and @cpp "sceNetResolverDestroy()" @ce
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (leaks one resolver handle per lookup, emulator only) @m_endspan are harmless.
Vita3K re-runs a cached image of an @cpp "eboot.bin" @ce it has loaded before, so to test a new binary
quit the emulator fully or give each build its own title id with `-D VITA_TITLEID=`.

@subsection consoles-vita-shaders The console needs a firmware module

The VitaSDK has no offline shader compiler, so shaders are compiled <b>on the console</b> by
@cpp "libshacccg.suprx" @ce from the firmware, whichever backend is used. Extract it following
[VitaShaRK's instructions](https://github.com/Rinnegatamante/vitaShaRK) and place it in
@cpp "ur0:/data/" @ce.

-   `GXM` ships <b>Cg source</b> @m_span{m-text m-dim} (generated @cpp "CgGeneratedShaders.h" @ce,
    see @ref shader-compiler-cg) @m_endspan and compiles it through vitaShaRK when a program links.
    Without the module the backend refuses to start and says so in the log.
-   `OpenGL` passes GLSL to @cpp glCompileShader() @ce, which vitaGL compiles through the same
    SceShaccCg --- so external @cpp ".shader" @ce files and rescale filters work here too.

@subsection consoles-vita-shadercache The compiled shaders are cached, and shipped

On-console compilation costs about 3.3 s of a cold start, so
@cpp nCine::RHI::GXM::GxmShaderCache @ce keeps the GXP binaries SceShaccCg produces.

-   Entries are keyed by a 64-bit hash of the exact Cg source, so only changed stages recompile. The
    file header holds a format revision and a fingerprint of @cpp "libshacccg.suprx" @ce; either
    mismatch rejects the whole pack and the console recompiles.
-   <b>Two packs are loaded</b>: the read-only @cpp "app0:/Content/Shaders/ShadersGxm.bin" @ce shipped
    in the VPK, then the writable one under @cpp "ux0:/data/jazz2/Cache/Shaders/" @ce over it. Writes
    only go to the writable one.
-   To refresh the shipped pack, run once on a console, pull the written pack back and commit it to
    `Sources/Shaders/Prebaked/Vita/ShadersGxm.bin` (packaged for Vita only). <b>Commit it exactly as
    the console wrote it</b> --- some keys belong to sources not in @cpp "CgGeneratedShaders.h" @ce
    (runtime-patched batched shaders, the clear/present shaders in @cpp "GxmDevice.cpp" @ce), and
    pruning deletes them. See its `README.md`.
-   The pack holds 65 binaries; 53 load at startup, the other 12 are the x-ray and blur-behind
    stages compiled only when a level uses them @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see
    @ref Jazz2::ContentResolver::CompileLayerEffectShaders()) @m_endspan. The refresh run must
    therefore play such a level --- the LayerFX test level in `Sources/Jazz2/Tests` does.

@subsection consoles-vita-batching Sprite batching

The backend publishes @cpp IRhiCapabilities::IntValues::MaxBatchSize @ce as <b>32</b>, bounding both
the `BATCH_SIZE` baked into the batched Cg programs and @cpp nCine::RenderBatcher @ce. Only this
ceiling must match the shader; @cpp minBatchSize @ce stays 2, since raising it to the ceiling would
draw every shorter run one command at a time.

@subsection consoles-vita-limits Limits and known issues

<b>Rendering</b>

-   The ES 2.0 profile has no UBOs and no @cpp gl_VertexID @ce, so its shaders use the <b>ESSL 100</b>
    lowering from ShaderCompiler @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref shader-compiler-platforms) @m_endspan.
    `GXM` also lacks a vertex-ID input and reuses the same rewrite, reading the quad corner from a
    vertex attribute.
-   <b>sceGxm's polygon mode must match the primitive</b>: under the default
    @cpp SCE_GXM_POLYGON_MODE_TRIANGLE_FILL @ce, @cpp SCE_GXM_PRIMITIVE_LINES @ce draws nothing.
    @cpp GxmDevice::DrawCommon() @ce selects `LINE`, `POINT` or `TRIANGLE_FILL` from the primitive;
    since it is sticky state, the paths bypassing it (stencil band, @cpp Clear() @ce, present blit)
    set `TRIANGLE_FILL` themselves.
-   <b>Consecutive scenes are not ordered on the GPU</b>, so a scene can sample a render target an
    earlier scene has not finished (a flickering player light). @cpp GxmDevice::FinishScene() @ce
    waits for those scenes before submitting; scene dependency flags or a shared sync object don't
    replace this wait.
-   <b>Buffers are never orphaned</b>: they are GPU-visible memory written in place
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp nCine::RHI::GXM::GxmBufferObject @ce) @m_endspan and re-declared by
    @cpp nCine::RenderBuffersManager::Remap() @ce while scenes may still read them, so only a fresh
    block may be cleared.
-   Under <b>Vita3K</b>, `GXM` shows a one-pixel seam where a repeating texture wraps; it is an
    emulator artifact, no workaround is applied.
-   <b>Rendering Resolution</b> @m_span{m-text m-dim} (Options > Graphics) @m_endspan: `GXM` renders
    the whole frame into a smaller surface that @ref nCine::RHI::GXM::GxmDevice::PresentFrame()
    stretches onto the 960x544 panel. Choices are 50% (480x272, default, see
    @ref nCine::RHI::GXM::GxmDevice::ScreenWidth), 60% (576x326) and 75% (720x408), resized live via
    @ref nCine::RHI::GXM::GxmDevice::ResizeScreenSurface(). On other shader-tier platforms the option
    scales the 720x405 view bound instead; direct-tier consoles don't offer it.
-   <b>Blur effects and high-quality water are on by default</b>, using the cheaper `LOW_POWER_GPU`
    shader paths @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref shader-compiler-input-preprocessor) @m_endspan.
-   <b>Shorter blur chain</b>, since each pass is a separate scene that must finish first: below 100%
    the first blur pass reads the view directly; at 25% and below the quarter-size level is dropped too
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@ref Jazz2::Rendering::PlayerViewport::Initialize(), all platforms) @m_endspan.
-   <b>X-ray and blur-behind layers cost about 4--6 ms</b> where several are on screen, mostly from
    extra scenes.
-   <b>The firmware's Cg compiler is stricter than Vita3K's</b> (e.g. <em>ambiguous call to 'lerp'</em>;
    see @ref shader-compiler-cg), and one failed program reverts every special layer to its reduced form
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref Jazz2::ContentResolver::CompileLayerEffectShaders()) @m_endspan.
    Compile a new shader on a console once before trusting it --- refreshing the shipped pack does this.

<b>Networking</b>

-   <b>Online multiplayer works, over ENet only.</b> `WITH_WEBSOCKET` is forced off (VitaSDK lacks
    @cpp "netinet/ip.h" @ce, which IXWebSocket includes). `ENET_IPV6=0` selects ENet's IPv4 arm, as
    on the PSP and Switch --- @cpp "psp2/net/net.h" @ce has no IPv6. ENet's unused
    @cpp "sys/ioctl.h" @ce include is skipped on this target.
-   <b>VitaSDK's `"inet_pton()"` is not POSIX</b>: it returns -1 instead of 0 for a host name. This
    broke HTTPS (libcurl/OpenSSL failed every request with @cpp "no alternative certificate subject
    name matches target ipv4 address" @ce) and ENet's @cpp "enet_address_set_host_ip()" @ce. It is
    replaced in @cpp "Sources/nCine/Backends/Vita/VitaLibcCompat.cpp" @ce.
-   <b>`"sceNetSendmsg()"` caps the number of iovecs</b> well below the 65 ENet may use per datagram;
    a 33-buffer send returns `EMSGSIZE` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (SCE error 0x28) @m_endspan and ENet
    drops the connection @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp "enet_host_service() returned -1" @ce, shortly
    after joining) @m_endspan. The Vita uses the gather-then-@cpp "sendto()" @ce arm like the 3DS and
    PSP. Failed sends, receives and socket options log `errno` and @cpp "*sceNetErrnoLoc()" @ce.
-   No explicit network bring-up is needed: newlib calls @cpp "_vita_net_init()" @ce lazily. Its pool
    is small (about 140 KB vs. ENet's 256 KB socket buffers) --- suspect it first if the transport ever
    runs out of buffers.

<b>Other</b>

-   Asynchronous tracing is disabled; threads are available.
-   Sound needs the SDK to provide OpenAL; without it, audio is compiled out.
-   <b>Module music is libopenmpt</b>, built from source at configure time. `NCINE_WITH_XMP=ON` uses
    the SDK's prebuilt <b>libxmp</b> instead @m_span{m-text m-dim} (no download, several MB smaller)
    @m_endspan, at the cost of the four `.mo3` tracks and some playback accuracy.

- - -

@section consoles-switch Nintendo Switch

A shader platform built with <b>devkitA64</b>, using <b>SDL2</b> and desktop OpenGL. Install the
`switch-dev` group and use @cpp "$DEVKITPRO/cmake/Switch.cmake" @ce:

@code{.sh}
sudo dkp-pacman -S --needed switch-dev
cmake -B ./build/switch/ -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -D CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=${DEVKITPRO}/cmake/Switch.cmake \
    -D DEATH_TRACE_ASYNC=OFF
make -j $(nproc) -C ./build/switch/
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

`DEATH_TRACE_ASYNC` must be off --- it crashes on startup here. The build produces
@cpp "build/switch/jazz2.nro" @ce with the game content <b>embedded in its RomFS</b>: copy the `.nro`
to @cpp "sd:/switch/" @ce and start it from `hbmenu`. Like the Vita, the Switch converts the original
game data itself --- put an original installation into @cpp "sdmc:/Games/Jazz2/Source/" @ce; the cache
and save data appear in @cpp "sdmc:/Games/Jazz2/" @ce.

- - -

@section consoles-libretro Libretro core

Not a console target itself, but a way to reach console-like devices: with `NCINE_BUILD_LIBRETRO=ON`
the game builds as a <b>libretro core</b> @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp "jazz2_libretro.so" @ce, sources in
@cpp "Sources/nCine/Backends/Libretro" @ce) @m_endspan driven by RetroArch, with no window backend of its
own. `NCINE_PREFERRED_RHI` can be:

-   `Software` --- the framebuffer is handed to `retro_video_refresh`; works on every frontend
-   `OpenGL` --- renders into the frontend's FBO via `SET_HW_RENDER`, targeting OpenGL\|ES 3.0

@code{.sh}
cmake -B ./build/libretro/ -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -D NCINE_BUILD_LIBRETRO=ON -D NCINE_PREFERRED_RHI=Software
make -j $(nproc) -C ./build/libretro/
@endcode

- - -

@section consoles-ci Continuous integration

Each console is built on every push by its own workflow in @cpp ".github/workflows" @ce, inside the
SDK's container image, and uploads a ready-to-deploy package:

Workflow | Container image | Artifact
-------- | --------------- | --------
`dreamcast.yml` | `pcercuei/dreamcast-toolchain` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (compilers only; KOS, kos-ports and mkdcdisc are built in the job and cached) @m_endspan | `Jazz2.cdi` + `Jazz2.elf`
`wii.yml` | `devkitpro/devkitppc` | the `sd/` card tree with `apps/Jazz2/boot.dol`
`gamecube.yml` | `devkitpro/devkitppc` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (AssetPacker built in the job for the disc image) @m_endspan | `Jazz2.iso` + the `sd/` card tree with `Jazz2/Jazz2.dol`
`3ds.yml` | `devkitpro/devkitarm` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (installs `3ds-zlib`, `3ds-curl`, `3ds-mbedtls`) @m_endspan | the `sdmc/` card tree with `3ds/Jazz2/Jazz2.3dsx`
`psp.yml` | `pspdev/pspdev` | the `ms0/` memory-stick tree with `EBOOT.PBP`
`ps2.yml` | `ps2dev/ps2dev` | `Jazz2.iso` + `Jazz2.elf` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (for PCSX2's `-elf`) @m_endspan
`vita.yml` | `vitasdk/vitasdk` | `Jazz2.vpk`
`switch.yml` | `devkitpro/devkita64` | `Jazz2.nro`

@m_class{m-noindent}

-   The PlayStation 3 has <b>no workflow</b> --- there is no official SDK container; see
    @ref consoles-ps3-toolchain for the manual setup.
-   KallistiOS, kos-ports and mkdcdisc in `dreamcast.yml` are <b>pinned to specific commits</b> and
    cached against them, so upstream changes cannot break the build or the runtime.
-   The workflows use the repository's own @cpp "Content" @ce (they cannot ship original game data),
    so their artifacts boot to the main menu only; prepare a content tree as in
    @ref consoles-common-content to play a level.

- - -

@section consoles-troubleshooting Troubleshooting

-   <b>The game starts, but no episode can be played.</b> The content tree is missing, incomplete or
    not marked as prepared. Most likely: the tree was never passed as `NCINE_CONTENT_DIR`
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (a <b>cached</b> variable --- passing it to an already configured build
    directory does nothing) @m_endspan, the tree has @cpp "Source.pak" @ce instead of
    @cpp "Prebaked.pak" @ce @m_span{m-text m-dim} (a `desktop` profile conversion) @m_endspan, or it
    is not named @cpp "Content" @ce on the medium. See @ref consoles-common-content.
-   <b>The menu itself is unreadable or empty.</b> The conversion didn't get the game's own content
    (fonts, UI graphics, @cpp "Metadata" @ce, translations) and warned <em>"No game content directory
    was found beside the original files"</em>. Pass @cpp "--content=<dir>" @ce pointing at the
    repository's @cpp "Content" @ce, see step 2 of @ref consoles-common-content.
-   <b>`No "Source.pak" in …, the disc image will not be marked as prebaked` at configure time</b>
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (PS2, N64) @m_endspan is <b>expected</b> with the `console` profile, which
    already names it @cpp "Prebaked.pak" @ce, see @ref consoles-ps2-content. Only a problem if neither
    package is in the tree.
-   <b>A file removed from the content tree is still on the disc</b> (Dreamcast, PS2). The staging
    directory is never cleared: delete @cpp "<build dir>/cd/" @ce and rebuild, see
    @ref consoles-dreamcast-build.
-   <b>The intro cinematic crawls on the Dreamcast.</b> The tree was built with `--target=console`
    instead of `--target=dreamcast`, see @ref consoles-dreamcast-content.
-   <b>Text is missing, tilesets look wrong, or a level refuses to load.</b> Usually a <b>stale</b>
    content tree --- regenerate it with an AssetPacker built from current sources. On Wii/GameCube make
    sure the SD card (or emulator image) was really rewritten.
-   <b>No CDI image after a Dreamcast build.</b> `mkdcdisc` was not on `PATH` when CMake configured,
    see @ref consoles-dreamcast-build.
-   <b>An empty log.</b> Enable the console's receiver --- Flycast's serial console, a USB Gecko in
    slot B on the PowerPC consoles, PPSSPP's stdout. On the Dreamcast and PSP startup messages go to
    the screen, not the log.
-   <b>`std::bad_alloc`, an `sbrk` message, or an unexplained CPU exception</b> on the Dreamcast or
    GameCube means main memory ran out; `Out of PVR memory allocating …` on the Dreamcast is video
    memory, see @ref consoles-dreamcast-limits.
-   <b>PPSSPP runs an old build.</b> It boots from its own configured memory stick, see
    @ref consoles-psp-deploy.
-   <b>CMake cannot find the compiler on the Dreamcast.</b> @cpp "environ.sh" @ce was not sourced in
    that shell, see @ref consoles-dreamcast-toolchain.
-   <b>A fully loaded sound is cut short on the Dreamcast.</b> An AICA channel addresses at most 65534
    samples, see @ref consoles-dreamcast-limits.
-   <b>Sound effects are noise on the Wii or GameCube.</b> Samples were emitted little-endian; buffer
    formats are native-endian, see @ref consoles-common-audio.
-   <b>"Dead FIFO commands queue state" in RPCS3.</b> Usually the emulator (headless run, no GPU,
    swapping), see @ref consoles-ps3-deploy. If genuine, memory the GPU is still reading was reused or
    the command ring wrapped without a flush.
-   <b>A shader reports "has no compiled RSX microcode" on the PS3.</b> The `vp40`/`fp40` profiles
    rejected that variant when the table was generated, see @ref consoles-ps3-limits.
-   <b>`ppu-gcc` refuses to run</b> with "environment variable 'PSL1GHT' not defined". Export it in
    the build shell, see @ref consoles-ps3-toolchain.
-   <b>The PS3 build does not link</b>, with `DeflateStream` unresolved. The portlibs (zlib) were not
    built, see @ref consoles-ps3-toolchain.

*/

}
